User: I'm going through a legal process and need to organize my thoughts. The case involves landlord-tenant eviction case. My lawyer is Maria Santos from Santos Law Office; we have proceedings at small claims court. The discovery phase just ended and we're waiting for a hearing date.. Opposing party: the other parent in the custody case. This is in San Diego County, California. I have documentation but I'm not sure what else the court will want. Can you help me figure out what to ask my attorney about what questions the judge or opposing counsel might ask? I want to be ready for the legal next steps.
Assistant: Good — I can help you get organized and make sure you ask your attorney the right, focused questions so you’re prepared for the next steps. Below I’ve put together:

- A short checklist of documents/evidence the court is likely to want
- A set of tailored questions to ask Maria Santos (grouped by topic)
- Likely questions a judge might ask and how to prepare brief answers
- Likely lines of questioning from opposing counsel and how to handle them
- Practical next steps and a suggested plan (including offers to draft an email or run a mock Q&A)

If you want, I can also turn the questions into an email to Maria Santos, or role-play the judge/opposing counsel so you can practice answers.

Documents / evidence checklist (gather these now)
- Lease/rental agreement and any written amendments
- Notices served (pay-or-quit, cure-or-quit, termination, 3/30/60-day notices) and proof of service
- Rent ledger / payment history (bank records, cancelled checks, money orders, receipts)
- Demand letter(s) and correspondence (texts, emails, landlord/tenant apps)
- Photographs and video (with dates) showing condition of the unit, damages, repairs
- Repair invoices, estimates, receipts for repairs or replacements
- Move-in/move-out checklist and condition reports, security deposit receipts/return
- Police reports, incident reports, or 911 records (if relevant)
- Inspection reports (city/health/building), notices from government agencies
- Discovery materials and any declarations or affidavits you produced or received
- Witness names and short statements (neighbors, contractors, maintenance personnel)
- Court filings you/they have already filed, and any orders or prior judgments
- Any evidence relevant to damages you claim (receipts for alternative housing, lost property)
- Chronology/timeline of events (one-page summary with dates)

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped)

Case fundamentals / legal standards
- Exactly what legal claims/causes of action will be decided at the hearing? (eviction/unlawful detainer, money, damages, possession)
- What elements do we need to prove for each claim? What is the legal standard the judge will apply?
- What defenses is the opposing party likely to assert?
- Is our case actually in small claims or in superior court (unlawful detainer is usually in superior court)? Can you confirm whether you (or I) can have counsel appear?

Evidence / exhibits
- Which documents and exhibits are most persuasive for each element of our case?
- Do exhibits need to be pre-marked or exchanged with the court/opponent beforehand? If so, what format/number of copies?
- How should I authenticate documents (witness, affidavit, business records foundation)?
- Are any documents likely to be excluded? How do we deal with hearsay or relevance objections?

Witnesses & testimony
- Which witnesses should we call and what will each witness say that helps prove our case?
- Do we need written witness declarations or sworn affidavits? Should witnesses be subpoenaed now?
- Should I prepare a short direct-examination script or bullet-point answers for each witness?

What the judge will ask
- What procedural questions will the judge likely ask at the hearing (e.g., proof of service, jurisdiction, standing, timeline)?
- What factual questions is the judge most likely to focus on in our type of eviction case?
- How long will we have to present our case? Can you give me an estimate of the time for direct and cross?

What opposing counsel might ask / pitfalls
- What are the vulnerable facts in my case the opposing party will try to exploit?
- What hostile or leading questions should I expect, and how should I answer them succinctly?
- Are there admissions I should avoid making? What should I not say on the stand?

Procedural & logistics
- What is the expected hearing date window and how will we be notified?
- Who will appear at the hearing (judge, commissioner); is there a pretrial conference?
- How many exhibits can we present, and do we need to file any pretrial statement?
- Do we need to bring separate copies for the judge, the courtroom clerk, opposing counsel, and ourselves?
- What courtroom etiquette should I follow (arrival time, dress, opening statement, time limits)?
- What are our settlement options and how should we evaluate a settlement offer?

Strategy & outcomes
- What is our realistic best- and worst-case outcome?
- What damages can I claim and how do we calculate them (back rent, damages, relocation costs)?
- What is the cost/benefit of going to hearing vs. trying to settle or mediate?
- If we lose, what are the appeal or post-judgment options and deadlines?

Preparing YOU to testify
- Can we do a mock Q&A so I practice concise answers and avoid volunteering extra information?
- What are the three most important points I should make when speaking to the judge?
- How should I respond to leading or aggressive questioning from opposing counsel?

San Diego-specific / court rules
- Are there any San Diego County local rules or forms I must complete before the hearing?
- Does this courthouse have time limits or special evidence protocols for eviction/small claims hearings?
- Do we need to worry about remote appearance rules or tech requirements (if virtual)?

Likely questions a judge might ask (practice answers)
- "Can you briefly explain what relief you’re asking for and why?" — Prepare a 30–60 second statement: what you want (possession, rent, damages), and 3 key facts proving it.
- "When did the tenancy start and what are the key dates (notice served, nonpayment dates)?" — Have dates memorized in chronological order.
- "Did you serve the proper notice? How and when was it served?" — Show proof of service and say the method (personal, substituted, mailed).
- "Did you make efforts to mitigate damages or work with the tenant/other party?" — Be ready to list steps you took (repair attempts, communications, offering payment plan).
- "Are there any prior agreements or court orders that affect this case?" — Have any orders or agreements handy and know what they say.

Likely lines of questioning from opposing counsel (and how to handle them)
- Questions attacking credibility: “Isn’t it true you did X?” — Answer truthfully but briefly; correct inaccuracies without volunteering extra.
- Questions about unrelated personal matters (e.g., custody issues): Redirect to relevance; ask your attorney whether and how to respond. Don’t elaborate on unrelated disputes unless they’re directly relevant.
- Questions about amounts/damages: Have receipts and documentation ready; don’t estimate — refer to documents where possible.
- Rapid-fire questions: Pause, take a breath, and answer one question at a time. Ask to repeat or clarify if you don’t understand.

Practical preparation plan (next 2–3 weeks)
1. Compile and organize exhibits in a binder or labeled digital folder (tabbed by topic: lease, notices, payments, communications, photos).
2. Create a one-page factual timeline with dates and short notes — keep this to 1 page for quick reference in court.
3. Prepare exhibit list and copies (one for judge, one for opposing counsel, one for your attorney, one for yourself).
4. Identify and prepare witnesses; obtain written statements or affidavits if helpful. Consider subpoenas if necessary — ask attorney to prepare them now.
5. Schedule a prep session with Maria Santos: run through direct testimony, opposing counsel’s likely lines, and courtroom procedure.
6. Confirm hearing logistics with the court and your attorney (date/time, location, length, remote appearance rules).
7. Discuss settlement posture and any reserve amounts you’d accept.

Suggested phrasing for key questions to Maria Santos (copy/paste)
- “Maria, please confirm the exact legal claims and elements we must prove at the hearing.”
- “Which documents and witnesses do you believe are essential to submit, and do we need to pre-file or exchange exhibits?”
- “Can we do a mock direct and cross-examination session so I can practice concise answers?”
- “Are there any San Diego County-specific procedures, forms, or time limits we must follow?”
- “What settlement options should I consider and what would be a reasonable bottom-line to accept?”

Safety/nuisance/legal boundaries
- If the opposing party’s behavior has been threatening or harassing, tell your attorney immediately; ask about protective orders or safety steps for court.

Would you like me to:
- Draft an email to Maria Santos with the list of questions above? OR
- Convert the list into a one-page checklist you can bring to the hearing? OR
- Role-play the judge or opposing counsel with likely Q&A so you can practice responses?

Tell me which of those you prefer (or any additional details about your case: exact relief sought, whether Maria will appear at the hearing, and any dates) and I’ll prepare the next item.

