A. Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case status & timeline
  - What is the expected timeline from discovery close to the hearing and possible trial date? When will we be notified of the hearing date?
  - Are there any remaining pre-hearing filings or motions we should expect or need to file?
- Jurisdiction & procedure
  - Is this being heard in small claims, limited civil, or superior court? Will you represent me at the hearing or only advise? (If it’s small claims, can I have counsel in court?)
  - Are there any local San Diego County rules or eviction ordinances we must follow (rent control, relocation assistance, notice requirements)?
- Evidence & proof
  - Which documents are essential to prove my case (and in what order should they be presented)?
  - Do we need originals, certified copies, or can we use photocopies? Should anything be notarized?
  - How many copies of exhibits should I bring for the judge, opposing counsel, and ourselves?
- Notices & service
  - Do we have proper proof of service for all notices (e.g., 3‑day pay/quit, 30/60-day notices)? Are those proofs sufficient and admissible?
- Damages and remedies
  - What exact damages or remedies should I ask the court for (past-due rent, possession, costs, attorney’s fees, damages to property, filing fees)?
  - How will damages be calculated and documented?
- Defenses and counterclaims
  - What defenses might the opposing party raise or what counterclaims could they assert (retaliation, discrimination, wrongful lockout, breach of quiet enjoyment)?
  - How should we respond if they raise habitability/repair claims or a retaliation defense?
- Witnesses & testimony
  - Do we need witnesses? If so, who (property manager, witnesses to incidents, contractors, neighbors)? How should they be prepared?
  - Will written witness statements or declarations be acceptable, or is live testimony preferable?
- Settlement & negotiation
  - Should we attempt settlement before the hearing? What is our bottom line?
  - If settlement is reached, what form should it take (written stipulation, consent judgment) to protect my interests?
- Practical/strategic
  - Are there any motions you recommend now (summary judgment, exclude evidence, continuance)?
  - What are the risks of losing and what is the appeals/collection process if we win/lose?
  - What costs and fees should I expect (court costs, potential appeals, collection)?

B. Questions the judge or opposing counsel are likely to ask
- Procedural / jurisdictional questions
  - Were proper notices served? When and how were they served? (Judge wants specifics: date, method, who served.)
  - When did the tenancy begin and what are the relevant lease terms?
- Facts about rent & breach
  - How much rent is owed and for what periods? Do you have a rent ledger or bank records?
  - Did the tenant cure the breach after notice? If payments were missed, were any partial payments made?
- Proof & timing
  - Can you show the lease, notices, receipts, photographs, repair invoices, communications, and proof of service?
  - Who prepared these documents and how do you authenticate them?
- Habitability / repair claims (if raised)
  - Were repair requests made? When were they made and how did you respond?
  - Were any repairs necessary due to the tenant’s actions vs normal wear and tear?
- Credibility / inconsistencies
  - Why are there differences between your timeline and the opposing party’s timeline?
  - Have there been prior similar disputes or related court filings (e.g., custody issues mentioned)? (Judge may probe relevance.)
- Mitigation or alternative remedies
  - Did you try to mitigate damages (e.g., re-rent the unit)? If so, what steps were taken and what were the results?
