  - Undermining your timeline or documentary proofs (point out contradictions, missing dates, or unsigned docs)
  - Demonstrating failure to follow local eviction notice/tenant-protection rules
  - Raising counterclaims like retaliation or failure to maintain the unit

C. Documents and exhibits to have organized (bring originals + copies)
- Lease or rental agreement (signed pages)
- Notice(s) served: 3‑day pay/quit, 30/60-day notices, termination notices — with proof of service (signed receipt, certified mail, process server affidavit)
- Rent ledger / ledger printouts, bank statements, canceled checks, receipts showing payments and arrears
- Photographs and videos (dated or timestamped) showing property condition or damages
- Repair invoices, estimates, contracts, receipts (showing cost to repair damage)
- Copies of all written communications (texts, emails, letters) between parties — organized chronologically
- Any police reports or incident reports (if relevant)
- Witness contact information and short witness statements/declarations prepared in advance
- Any prior court documents or discovery responses (interrogatories, admissions, deposition excerpts)
- Notice of intent to withhold or other statutory notices (if applicable under CA law)
- Identification and contact info for any contractors or inspectors
- A one-page chronology/timeline of events to hand to counsel (and bring extra copies for the judge if permitted)

D. Practical prep and strategy
- Create a concise timeline (one page) with dates and one-line facts — this helps both your lawyer and the judge quickly grasp the case.
- Label exhibits clearly (Exhibit A, B, C...) and create an exhibit list with short descriptions and where each came from.
- Prepare short witness summaries so each witness knows exactly the facts they will be asked to testify about.
- Practice answering common questions succinctly: who, what, when, where, why. Courts favor concise, factual answers.
- Ask your attorney to pre-empt expected weaknesses (e.g., missing signatures, gaps in notices) and how to address them at hearing.
- Discuss settlement thresholds with your attorney now so you can respond quickly if settlement is offered before the hearing.
- Confirm logistics: what time to arrive, how long to expect the hearing to take, courtroom rules for exhibits and electronics, parking, and whether any remote appearance option exists.
- Discuss collection strategy in advance — winning a judgment is one step; collecting can be a separate process (wage garnishment, writs of possession, levies).

E. Questions about the opposing party’s status and other litigation
- Because you mentioned the opposing party is “the other parent in the custody case,” confirm with Maria whether any custody-related matters or family court orders could impact or overlap with the eviction proceeding (privacy, service, jurisdictional conflicts).
