First, a quick clarification request
- When you say “eviction” and “small claims court,” do you mean this matter was actually filed in small claims? (Unlawful detainer/eviction cases are usually in Superior Court, not small claims.) Also, can Maria attend or represent you at the hearing, or is she only preparing you? California has limits on attorney representation in small claims, so confirm that with her.

Below is a practical, organized checklist you can use with Maria Santos to get ready. It’s divided into (A) questions to ask your attorney, (B) documents/evidence to gather and how to present them, (C) sample questions the judge may ask, (D) sample questions opposing counsel might ask, and (E) courtroom / answer strategies and next-step logistics.

A. Key questions to ask your attorney
- Case status & venue
  - Is this actually a small claims matter or an unlawful detainer in Superior Court? How does that change procedure and remedies?
  - What is the likely timeline from hearing to judgment and to enforcement?
- Attorney’s role
  - Can you (Maria) appear in court on my behalf, or will you only prepare me? If you cannot appear, can you sit behind me or advise me on the day?
  - What support will you provide at hearing (exhibit prep, witness prep, trial script)?
- Legal theory & burden
  - What exactly must I prove to win (breach of lease, unpaid rent, proper notice, habitability, etc.)?
  - What defenses might the other side raise and how do we counter them?
- Evidence & admissibility
  - Which documents are most important and how should they be authenticated?
  - Do we need written witness declarations or live witnesses? Do we need subpoenas for any documents or witnesses?
  - Are there any hearsay or authentication issues I should worry about?
- Notices & procedural compliance
  - Did I properly serve the required notice(s) (3-day pay/quit, 30/60, cure/quit, etc.)? If there’s any doubt, how will that affect our case?
- Weaknesses & strategy
  - What are our case’s main weaknesses and the best ways to minimize them?
  - What’s our settlement posture? When should we consider settling and at what terms?
- Damages and remedies
  - Exactly what relief should I seek (past rent, future rent, damages, security deposit return, court costs, small claims limits)?
  - How are costs and fees handled here? Can I recover filing costs? Attorney fees?
- Hearing logistics
  - How long will I have to present my case? What order should evidence/witnesses be presented in?
  - How many copies of exhibits should I bring, and in what format (exhibit binder, pads, electronic)?
  - What will the judge expect on hearing day (dress, arrival time, check-in procedure)?
- Post-judgment and enforcement
  - If we win, how do we enforce the judgment (writs, garnishment, levy)? If we lose, what are appeal options or other remedies?
- Specific questions about interactions
  - How should I answer questions from opposing counsel or the judge if they ask something we don’t have an immediate answer for?
  - Are there phrases or admissions I should avoid saying in court?

B. Documents and evidence checklist (bring originals + organized copies)
- Lease / rental agreement (signed copy)
- Notices served (3/30/60-day, pay/quit, termination): copies with proof of service (signed receipt, certified mail, process server declaration)
- Rent ledger & payment receipts (bank records, money orders, Venmo, checks) showing amounts/dates
- Communications: emails, texts, letters, repair requests, messages with dates (print and include metadata if possible)
- Photographs/videos with dates and captions (move-in, damage, repairs, habitability issues)
- Repair invoices, estimates, receipts, contractor statements
- Code enforcement notices, inspection reports, repair orders (if any)
- Move-in/move-out condition reports and security deposit documentation
- Witness statements or declarations (neighbors, contractors) — signed and dated
- Discovery responses, interrogatories, admissions, deposition transcripts from discovery phase
- Any prior court filings, pleadings, or orders in this matter
- Chronology/timeline: a one-page timeline of key dates (rent due, notices, communications, discovery events)
- Copies: for small claims, bring at least 3 sets of exhibits (judge, opposing party, your copy). Ask your attorney how many the court expects.

C. Sample questions the judge may ask (prepare short factual answers)
- What is your relationship to this property? (landlord/tenant)
- What remedy are you asking for? (specific dollar amount, possession, etc.)
- When did the tenancy start and what are rent terms?
- How much rent is owed and for what dates? Show proof.
- Did you provide the tenant with the required notice? When and how was it served?
- What attempts did you make to resolve this before filing (payment plans, communications)?
- Are there repair or habitability issues the tenant raised? What did you do about them?
- Has the tenant paid any rent since you filed? Have you accepted any partial payments?
- Are there any agreements or oral modifications to the lease?
- Do you have witnesses? Who will testify and about what?
- Have you suffered any other damages (property damage, cleaning, lock-out costs)?

D. Sample questions opposing counsel (or the other party) might ask you
- Did you accept rent after sending the notice? If so, when and why?
- How and when did you serve the 3-day/30-day notice? Who signed for it?
- Did you make repairs requested by the tenant? If not, why not?
- Have you violated any local ordinances (e.g., lockout, shutting utilities)?
- Did you ever threaten the tenant or retaliate for complaints?
- Why did you not follow [specific step] when terminating the lease?
- Can you prove the exact amount claimed? (expect detailed interrogation of amounts/dates)
- Do you have any written lease amendments or verbal agreements?
- Were you aware of any disabilities or protected statuses that might affect eviction?
- For credibility: Have you ever been sued for similar matters before?

E. How to prepare answers and behave in court
- Short, factual answers: keep responses brief and factual. Don’t volunteer extra information.
- Refer to exhibits: Practice saying: “As shown in Exhibit 2, rent was unpaid for June and July” or “See Exhibit A, the certified mail receipt.”
- Avoid arguing with opposing counsel or the judge. If you need to clarify, ask permission: “May I finish my answer?” or “With your permission, I’ll refer to Exhibit 4.”
- Rehearse a 2–3 minute opening statement: who you are, what you’re asking for, and three key facts.
- Prepare witness(es): tell them to answer succinctly, bring ID, know exhibit numbers, and avoid speculation.
- Authentication: make sure someone can authenticate photos, texts, or receipts (who took it, when).
- Practice cross-examination: think of the weak points and prepare concise responses that don’t concede unnecessary points.
- Handling tricky questions: if you truly don’t know, say “I don’t recall” or “I don’t have that document here” rather than guessing.

F. Practical next steps to ask Maria to do now
- Confirm venue and the exact hearing date/time and whether you must appear in person.
- Review and pre-mark exhibits and prepare copies for court/opponent.
- Draft short witness declarations or statements and notarize if appropriate.
- Prepare a hearing checklist and a 2–3 minute opening statement/script.
- Run a mock Q&A with you that covers likely judge/opposing counsel questions.
- Verify service proof (certified mail or process server) is in the record and admissible.
- Discuss settlement windows and a target settlement figure if you want to negotiate.

