  - Are we in small claims or in Superior Court for an unlawful detainer? If it’s Superior Court, what are the different next steps?
  - What is the expected timeline for a hearing/trial date and any deadlines I must meet now?
- Case status & strategy
  - Based on discovery, what are our strongest legal theories and likely weaknesses?
  - Do you recommend mediation or settlement talks before the hearing? If so, what are our bottom-lines?
- Evidence & proof
  - Which documents and exhibits are most persuasive for the judge here?
  - Do any documents need notarization or a specific foundation to be admissible?
  - Are there any discovery follow-ups we should have done or can still request?
- Witnesses & testimony
  - Who should testify for us? How many witnesses are realistic/necessary?
  - How should I prepare witnesses (what to expect, what to avoid saying)?
- Questions and cross-examination
  - What questions should I expect from opposing counsel and the judge?
  - What points should I avoid volunteering on the stand?
- Procedural tactics
  - Are there any pretrial motions we should file (motions in limine, to exclude evidence)?
  - Will the judge accept digital photos/emails/texts, and in what format?
- Remedies, costs, and enforcement
  - What remedies or damages are we asking for, and how will the judge calculate them?
  - If we win, how do we enforce the judgment (eviction, collection, garnishment)?
  - Who pays court costs and attorney’s fees in this process?
- Practical/Logistics
  - What time should I arrive, how long should I expect to be there, and what to bring?
  - Will the hearing be remote or in-person? If remote, what platform and technical steps?
  - How many copies of exhibits do we need for the court and opposing party?
- Risks & contingency
  - What are the realistic risks (losing, counterclaims, costs)?
  - If we lose, what are appeal or post-judgment options and timelines?

B. Likely questions from the judge (prepare succinct, factual answers)
- Are you the landlord/property owner? What is your relationship to the tenant/occupant?
- What is the name and address of the rental property and the lease start/end dates?
- On what grounds are you seeking eviction (nonpayment, lease violation, holdover, etc.)?
- When and how did you serve the required notice (e.g., 3-day pay or quit)? Provide dates and proof of service.
- How much rent is owed? Show rent ledger, receipts, partial payments, concessions.
- What efforts did you make to resolve the issue before filing (communications, notices, offers)?
- Were any repairs or habitability complaints raised? What did you do in response?
- Are there any prior agreements, oral modifications, or rent credits?
- Do you have written lease, addenda, or rule violations? (Have copies ready.)
- Are there any safety or domestic violence issues that affect the case?

C. Likely questions from opposing counsel (cross-exam style)
- Did you properly serve the notice? (Expect follow-up: who served it, proof.)
- Did you accept partial rent after serving notice or otherwise waive the notice?
- Have you ever violated the lease or failed to maintain the property?
- Did you retaliate or act in bad faith (timing, motive)?
- Have you asked the tenant for documentation supporting their defenses (repairs, payments)?
- If custody/family issues overlap: are there personal conflicts or motives behind filing?

D. Documents & evidence checklist (bring originals + copies)
- Lease or rental agreement and any addenda.
- Notices served (3-day, 30/60-day, cure notices) with proof of service (certified mail, process server, signed receipt).
- Rent ledger showing payments, dates, and balances.
- Receipts, canceled checks, bank statements proving payments.
- All written communications: emails, texts, letters (print in readable format with dates).
- Photographs or video (date-stamped if possible) showing property condition or damage.
- Repair invoices, estimates, contractor statements.
- Police reports, incident reports, or 911 call logs (if relevant).
- Witness contact info and short written witness statements (who they are and what they’ll testify to).
- Discovery-produced documents and interrogatory answers.
- Any prior court orders or related case documents (including the custody case if it impacts tenancy).
- Identification documents to verify your identity and ownership (deed, lease signature).

E. Witness preparation (what to practice)
- Have witnesses stick to facts they personally observed — dates, times, actions.
- Practice a short timeline summary each witness will give.
- Prepare for cross-exam: practice answering calmly, briefly, and honestly; do not volunteer extra information.
- Go over exhibits with each witness so they can authenticate documents/pictures.

F. Trial presentation tips
- Prepare a concise timeline (one page) of key dates and actions to give the judge.
- Label exhibits clearly (Exhibit 1, 2, etc.) and bring at least two copies: one for the court, one for opposing party (plus your copy).
- Plan a 2–3 minute opening summary of your claim and a short closing summary of requested relief.
- Be respectful and concise when answering questions. Do not argue with the judge.
- If unsure of an answer, say you don’t recall or ask to confirm with a document.

G. Settlement & negotiation questions to discuss with counsel
- What settlement offers should I accept or reject? What’s our walk-away value?
- Can we negotiate conditional terms (move-out date, repayment plan, confidentiality)?
- Should settlement be put on the record or in writing and filed with the court?

H. Post-hearing & enforcement (ask attorney)
- If we win, what is the timeline to enforce eviction and collect judgment?
- What steps are required if the opposing party appeals or files a cross-claim?
- How do we record the judgment or obtain a Writ of Possession (if appropriate)?

I. Behavior & courtroom etiquette reminders
- Be on time, dress neatly, and silence phones.
- Address the judge politely (“Your Honor”).
- Keep answers short. Don’t argue. Don’t interrupt opposing counsel or the judge.
- Bring a notebook to track what’s asked and any rulings.

J. Quick action checklist to bring to your attorney meeting
- Confirm which court (small claims vs Superior Court) and hearing date possibilities.
- Share a one-page timeline of events.
- Provide attorney with copies of all documents listed above.
- Ask attorney to pre-mark exhibits and file any required exhibit lists/Pretrial filings.
- Arrange witness availability for the hearing.
- Ask attorney to give you 3–5 practice questions and model answers for direct and cross-exam.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a concise email to Maria Santos listing these questions and ask for specific guidance and dates; or
- Create a one-page timeline template you can fill in and print for the hearing; or
- Make a short list of 8–10 practice Q&A for you to rehearse answers out loud.

