I can help you prepare. Below is a focused checklist of things to ask Maria Santos and a list of likely questions the judge or opposing counsel might ask — plus practical prep steps and a document checklist so you can show up organized and confident.

First — quick clarification I should flag
- Eviction (unlawful detainer) cases are normally handled in Superior Court (unlawful detainer), not small claims. Can you confirm whether your matter is actually set in Small Claims or in San Diego County Superior Court? If you’re unsure, ask Maria to confirm the court and department.

Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case status & strategy
  - What is our exact theory of the case and the relief we are asking the judge to grant?
  - What are the strengths and the biggest weaknesses of our case?
  - What is the likelihood of success and what are the realistic outcomes?
  - Do you recommend settlement and what terms would be acceptable?

- Evidence & admissibility
  - Which documents and pieces of evidence are most important? Are any documents risky (e.g., hearsay, unauthenticated)?
  - Do we need any additional evidence to prove elements (service of notice, possession, damages)?
  - Which documents should be pre-marked/exchanged and how many copies do I bring to court?
  - Are there any evidentiary objections we should raise or expect (hearsay, relevance, foundation)?

- Discovery & outstanding items
  - Is any discovery still outstanding or improperly answered by the other side?
  - Should we move to compel or file any motions in limine before the hearing?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Who should testify and in what order? Do we need any subpoenas for witnesses or records?
  - Will I testify, or will you handle the presentation? If I testify, what should I avoid saying?
  - Can we do a witness prep / mock direct and mock cross-examination session?

- Courtroom logistics & procedure
  - Confirm hearing date, time, courtroom/dept and estimated time limit.
  - What is the order of proceedings in this court? (opening, direct, cross, rebuttal)
  - How many copies of exhibits does the court require? Any local rules/forms?
  - Will the judge expect an exhibit list or binder in advance?

- Damages / calculations / relief
  - Exactly how should we calculate damages (unpaid rent, late fees, repairs, cleaning, filing costs)?
  - Do we have adequate proof for each monetary item and for any request for possession?

- Strategy for opposing counsel and the judge’s questions
  - What lines of questioning do you expect from opposing counsel?
  - What questions is the judge likely to ask so I can rehearse answers?

- Post-hearing questions
  - If we win, what are next steps for collection/possession? If we lose, what are appeal options/timelines?
  - What additional costs might we incur after judgment (collections, writ of possession)?

Likely questions the judge may ask (practice concise answers)
- Identity & role: “State your name and your role in this case (landlord/tenant).”
- Timeline: “When did the tenancy begin and end? What happened on [key dates]?”
- Notice & service: “When and how was the notice to pay/quit or terminate served?” (Have proof of service.)
- Amounts: “Exactly how much are you claiming and how was that number calculated?” (Bring a one-page damages summary.)
- Lease terms: “Was there a written lease? What did it require about rent, deposits, repairs?”
- Payments: “What payments were made and when? Do you have rent ledgers, bank statements, receipts?”
- Repairs/habitability (if tenant raises it): “Were there habitability issues? Did you make repairs or provide notice?”
- Mitigation: “What steps did you take to mitigate damages or find a replacement tenant?”
- Relationship/conflicts: “What is your relationship to the opposing party? Are there prior court orders (e.g., custody) that affect credibility?” (Be factual; don’t volunteer unrelated info.)
- Witness credibility: “Why should I believe your testimony over the other side’s?”

Common lines of questioning from opposing counsel (cross-exam themes)
- Credibility: challenge dates, memory, inconsistencies between statements/documents.
- Authenticity: question origin of documents, who prepared them, and whether they’re complete.
- Calculation disputes: attack amounts claimed as inflated or unsupported.
- Notice/service problems: argue notices were not properly served or were defective.
- Compliance with law: assert landlord didn’t follow legal steps (e.g., improper notice, failure to return deposit correctly).
- Mitigation: argue landlord didn’t attempt to re-rent or reduce damages.
- Motive/bias: bring up personal relationships (e.g., custody dispute) to suggest bias — be prepared to address neutrally.

Documents & evidence checklist (bring originals when possible + copies)
- Lease/rental agreement and any amendments
- Rent ledger / payment history (highlight missing payments)
- Notices (3-day, 30/60-day or other termination notices) and proof of service
- Proof of service for summons/complaint (or eviction notice)
- Security deposit receipts and accounting
- Photographs and videos (with dates/timestamps)
- Repair invoices, receipts, estimates and contractor statements
- Bank statements, cancelled checks, money transfer confirmations
- Emails, text messages, written communications between parties
- Police reports, code enforcement complaints, or inspection reports (if relevant)
- Witness statements or declarations
- Discovery responses, deposition transcripts, and any interrogatories
- Chronology/timeline: one-page timeline of key dates
- One-page damages summary showing math for the amount you claim

How to organize exhibits in court
- Pre-mark exhibits (Exhibit A, B, C...). Put a cover sheet and tab. Bring at least:
  - One copy for the judge
  - One copy for opposing counsel
  - One copy for yourself
- Provide an exhibit list and a short chronology to the judge and opposing counsel if allowed.
- Highlight relevant lines and keep originals accessible for verification.

Practical testimony tips
- Answer only the question asked. Keep answers short and factual.
- If you don’t know or don’t remember, say so — don’t guess.
- Practice a short 30–60 second summary of your case (what happened, relief sought, why you should win).
- Rehearse the numbers until you can state them precisely.
- Remain calm, polite, and professional even if provoked.

Practice prep to request from Maria
- A 30–60 minute mock direct/cross with her coaching answers.
- Review of the exhibit list and a determination of which exhibits might be objected to.
- Confirmation of who will speak in court (Maria or you) and how she’ll present the case.

Questions you can ask me to help prepare
- Would you like me to draft a concise email to Maria listing the questions above?
- Want a one-page timeline/damages summary template I can fill out with your facts?
- Want a mock Q&A script for likely judge and cross-examination questions so you can rehearse?

Next steps for you
1. Confirm whether this is small claims or Superior Court (unlawful detainer) and tell me the hearing date.
2. Confirm whether you are the landlord (plaintiff) or the tenant (defendant) so I can tailor the questions and sample answers.
3. Tell me whether you want a drafted email to Maria or a printable one-page hearing prep sheet (timeline + damages summary + exhibit list).

I can draft the email or create the one-page prep packet for you — tell me which and provide the basic facts (amount claimed, tenancy dates, main events, whether you will testify, and hearing date).

