I can help — below is a practical, organized plan you can bring to your attorney (Maria Santos) and use to rehearse. I’m not giving legal advice; this is to help you prepare questions and evidence so you and your lawyer can be ready for the hearing.

First — a couple quick clarifying questions
- Are you the landlord (plaintiff) or the tenant (defendant) in this matter?
- Is the case actually set in small claims court, or is it an unlawful detainer (eviction) in superior court? (Small claims and unlawful detainer have different rules and who can appear.)
- Do you expect to represent yourself at the hearing, or will someone from Santos Law Office appear/advise you at court?

Checklist of documents & items to bring to your attorney meeting and to court
- Case paperwork: complaint/answer, case number, court location, any notices from court about the hearing.
- Lease or rental agreement (all pages, any amendments).
- Rent ledger: every payment record (checks, bank statements, online transfer receipts), and dates missed/late.
- All notices: 3-day pay/quit, 30/60-day, cure notices — plus proof of service (signed receipts, certified mail, process server affidavit).
- Written communications: texts, emails, written notices between you and the other party.
- Photographs or video of the property (with dates), before/after photos if applicable.
- Repair invoices, receipts, contractor estimates, canceled checks for repairs.
- Inspection reports, municipal code violation notices, 311 complaints, police or incident reports (if any).
- Security deposit records: amounts collected, receipts, repair deductions, return notices.
- Witness list and contact info (neighbors, contractors, property manager) and short summary of what each will say.
- Any third-party reports (property manager logs, moving/storage receipts).
- A concise chronological timeline (one page) of the key events with dates.
- Copies: at least 3 copies of each exhibit (judge/CLERK, opposing party, your copy); a printed exhibit index (tabbed).

Likely procedural points to confirm with your attorney
- Which court and division (confirm judge or courtroom procedures for San Diego County).
- Whether Santos Law Office can appear for you (California small claims generally limits attorney appearance; unlawful detainer is different).
- Expected hearing length and time limits for each party’s presentation.
- Whether pre-marking exhibits or lodging copies with the court is required.
- Subpoena needs or deadlines for witness subpoenas and documents.
- Whether any recent local ordinances or moratoria affect your claim (San Diego specifics).

Questions the judge or opposing counsel are likely to ask (practice short, factual answers)
General:
- What is the remedy you are seeking? (money, possession, damages)
- What is your relationship to the property? (landlord/tenant)
- What is the timeline of tenancy and key events? (state exact dates, refer to timeline)
On notices and procedure:
- When and how did you serve the required notice? (date, method, proof of service)
- Did you follow statutory eviction procedures? (give documentary proof)
On rent/nonpayment:
- How much rent was owed? Show the math and ledger.
- Were partial payments accepted? If so, when and why?
On habitability/repairs and defenses (if tenant raises these or you as tenant raise them):
- What specific repairs were requested and when? Provide repair requests and responses.
- Were there code violations or intentionally withheld services? Provide reports/complaints.
Evidence/authenticity:
- How do you verify these documents are genuine? (explain origin and chain — bank statements, emails)
- Do you have eyewitnesses? What will they testify to?
Behavior and credibility:
- Did you attempt to resolve the dispute before filing? (dates and method of attempt)
- Any prior warnings, threats, or retaliatory acts? (keep answers factual)
If possession or move-out is contested:
- Who was in possession and when did possession change? (give dates and support)
- Were personal belongings handled appropriately? (receipts, storage records)

Specific tactical questions opposing counsel might ask (prepare short rebuttals)
- Why didn’t you accept a partial payment/offer? (state policy or agreement)
- Why weren’t repairs completed sooner? (provide reasons, receipts)
- Did you ever waive the breach by accepting payments? (point to written terms or lack thereof)
