I can help you get fully prepared. Below is a focused, practical list you can use when you talk with Maria Santos (and to rehearse before the hearing). If you want, I can also draft an email to Maria with these questions or create a mock Q&A for practice.

1) Short checklist to bring to the hearing
- Original lease(s) and any written amendments
- All notices (pay/quit, cure/quit, termination) with dates and proofs of service
- Rent ledger / payment history (bank records, checks, receipts)
- All communications with the opposing party (texts, emails, letters) with dates
- Photos or video (dated) of condition or damages
- Repair requests, invoices, receipts, work orders
- Police reports or code enforcement complaints (if relevant)
- Witness contact info and short written statements
- Any appraisal, estimate of damages, or receipts for repairs/cleaning
- Copies (x3–5) of everything for judge and opposing party
- Notebook with timeline of events (dates, short notes)

2) Questions to ask Maria Santos (organized)
Procedural / logistics
- What exact court (division, room) and hearing type are we waiting for? How long are these hearings typically?
- Are there local San Diego rules or court procedures I should know (exhibit numbering, time limits, how evidence is submitted)?
- Will you attend the hearing with me, or will you prepare me to present my case? (Clarify role: small claims rules may limit attorney courtroom representation—confirm.)
- How much time will I have to present my case? Are there limits on witnesses or documents?
- Who will testify first and what order is best?

Evidence & discovery follow-up
- Is there anything missing from our discovery responses that the court is likely to expect?
- Do you recommend any follow-up discovery, affidavits, or additional documentary proof?
- Which documents are most persuasive for the judge in eviction/unlawful detainer or small-claims eviction matters?

Legal issues & likely defenses
- What defenses might the opposing party raise (e.g., habitability/repair, payment, lease ambiguity, retaliation, landlord’s failure to mitigate)?
- How strong do you assess our case and which elements are we most vulnerable on?
- What legal standard will the judge apply for the relief we seek (possession, rent, damages)?

Witnesses & testimony
- Should we call any witnesses? If so, what should each witness be prepared to say?
- How should I testify—what facts should I emphasize and which to avoid?
- How should we handle potential cross-examination lines from opposing counsel?

Strategy & settlement
- Is settlement advisable before the hearing? What terms would be acceptable?
- If we settle, what form should the settlement take (written agreement, dismissal, payment schedule)?
- If the judge rules for us and the opposing party doesn’t comply, what are the enforcement steps and costs?

Practicalities & costs
- What court costs, filing fees, or other charges might I be responsible for?
- If we win, can we recover attorney’s fees or other costs? Under what circumstances?
- What are the deadlines for post-judgment remedies or appeals?

Custody/external-issues overlap (you mentioned the opposing party is the other parent)
- Could custody or family-court matters be used by the opposing party to undermine our position? How do we handle unrelated allegations?
- Are there confidentiality or safety concerns we should raise (restraining orders, sensitive info)?
- Should any custody-related documents be mentioned or excluded?

Contingencies & appeals
- If the judge rules against us, what are the likely next steps and timelines for appeal or relief?
- What evidence can we add post-judgment if necessary?

3) Questions the judge is likely to ask (practice short answers)
- What is your relationship to this property and what remedy are you seeking? (state: landlord/property owner; requesting possession, unpaid rent, damages—be specific)
- When did the tenancy start? What are the lease terms? (dates, rent amount, due date)
- What notices did you serve, when, and by what method? (have proofs)
- How much rent is owed and how did you calculate that number? (show ledger)
- What attempts did you make to resolve the issue before filing? (show communications)
- Did you try to mitigate damages (re-rent, notice period)? (explain efforts)
- Are there habitability or repair issues raised by the tenant? How were they addressed?
- Do you have witnesses or documents to support your claims? (offer exhibits)

4) Questions the opposing counsel (or opposing party) might ask you (practice and prepare short truthful answers)
- Did you accept partial payments? When and how much?
- Did you make any promises or agreements about rent/repairs orally?
- Why did you choose this particular date to serve notice or file suit?
- Have you tried to communicate or negotiate? (show timeline)
- Is there any prior history between us that affects this tenancy? (be factual; avoid emotional detail)
- How did you calculate damages/cleaning/repairs?

5) How to prepare testimony & handle cross-examination
- Keep answers short, factual, and date-oriented. Say only what you know.
- If you don’t remember or don’t know, say “I don’t recall” or “I don’t know,” then offer the record.
- Avoid volunteering extra information that isn’t asked.
- Practice a concise 60–90 second statement of your case: what happened, what you did, and what you want the court to order.
- Review potential inconsistencies in your documents ahead of time and be ready to explain them calmly.

6) Evidence organization tips
- Label exhibits (Exhibit 1: Lease; Exhibit 2: Notice to Quit; Exhibit 3: Rent ledger; etc.).
- Create an index of exhibits with short descriptions and dates.
- Provide copies to the judge and the opposing side per court rules.
- Highlight key pages/entries you intend to refer to.

7) San Diego / California small claims / eviction notes (check with Maria)
- Confirm whether this matter is actually in small claims or in Superior Court (evictions/unlawful detainer are usually in Superior Court).
- Confirm local court practices: how to submit exhibits, whether audio/video is allowed, proof of service requirements, and time limits for presentation.
- Ask Maria to confirm if she can represent you in-court or only prepare you (California small claims has limits on attorney representation).

8) Practice & role play
- Ask Maria for a short mock Q&A session where she cross-examines you on the key facts.
- Practice your 60–90 second opening statement with her feedback.
- Ask to rehearse answers to the specific judge questions above.

9) If you want me to draft an email to Maria
I can draft an email that lists the key questions and asks for next steps (hearing date, whether she will appear, document checklist, mock prep session). Tell me whether you want the tone brief/formal or detailed, and whether you want to include any attachments (timeline, documents list).

Would you like me to:
- Draft that email to Maria now, or
- Create a practice Q&A for you to rehearse?, or
- Help organize the specific exhibits and create an exhibit index template you can print?

