Good — this is a good moment to get organized. Below is a practical, actionable checklist you can use to prepare for your next steps and a list of focused questions to ask Maria Santos. I’ve grouped items so you can pick which you want to cover in a meeting or email.

First — one clarification to confirm with your lawyer
- Confirm the exact court and cause of action: “small claims” vs. unlawful detainer/superior court. (Evictions/unlawful detainer are usually handled in superior court; small claims handles money damages.) Ask Maria if the matter you’re describing will proceed in small claims or in another court. That affects procedure and what to expect.

Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case status & timeline
  - What are the likely next events and timeline (hearing date estimate, pretrial deadlines)?
  - Are there any outstanding motions or administrative steps we should expect?
- Attorney role & attendance
  - Will you attend the hearing with me? If not, what is your role at this stage?
  - If the hearing is in small claims, can you represent me in court or only advise? (Ask for clarification of the rules and whether you must appear alone.)
- Evidence & admissibility
  - Which documents do we need to have ready and in what form (originals, copies, certified copies)?
  - Are any items we gathered potentially inadmissible or weak? How can we strengthen them?
  - Do we need witness declarations or live witnesses? If so, how should they be prepared and served?
- Anticipated legal questions / lines of attack
  - What questions do you expect the judge to ask me or the other party?
  - What lines of questioning should I expect from opposing counsel?
  - What are the strongest and weakest parts of our case?
- Settlement & strategy
  - What settlement range is reasonable based on evidence and similar cases?
  - Should we attempt mediation/settlement before the hearing?
  - Would accepting a settlement affect any eviction timeline or custody-related issues?
- Remedies & enforcement
  - If we win, what remedies are available (money judgment, possession, writs)? How do we enforce them in San Diego County?
  - What are the chances of collecting? What are the post-judgment steps and costs?
- Costs, fees, & risks
  - What are expected court costs? Can we recover attorney fees or costs?
  - What defenses or counterclaims could the opposing party assert (e.g., retaliatory eviction, habitability, wrongful withholding of deposit)?
  - What are potential negative outcomes and how likely are they?
- Trial logistics
  - How long will the hearing likely last?
  - Do we need to exchange exhibit lists or serve anything before the hearing?
  - Can we appear virtually/remotely or must we appear in person? What are local courtroom rules and arrival details?

Possible questions the judge may ask (be ready to answer briefly and with documents)
- What relief are you asking for and how did you calculate the amount?
- What is the timeline of events? (lease start, missed rent dates, notices served, move-out date)
- Did you serve the required notices (e.g., 3-day pay-or-quit or other notice)? How and when were they served?
- What attempts did you make to resolve this before filing?
- Are you currently in possession of the property? Who is living there now?
- Do you have a written lease? What are its relevant terms (rent amount, due dates, security deposit)?
- Do you have proof of payment and the rent ledger?
- Are there any repairs, habitability issues, or police/inspection reports?
- Have you previously filed any related actions (e.g., small claims, unlawful detainer, custody matters that might be relevant)?
- Are you asking for any additional damages (late fees, repairs, cleaning, unpaid utilities)?

Questions opposing counsel (or the other parent) is likely to ask / lines of attack
- Challenge your timeline or memory: press for exact dates and documents.
- Dispute the amount claimed — ask for invoices, receipts, bank statements, ledgers.
- Argue lack of proper notice or procedural defects in how notices were served.
- Raise defenses: habitability/repair issues, retaliation, improper entry, breaches by landlord, or offsets for damages.
- Attack credibility: inconsistencies between testimony and documents, or between discovery responses and hearing testimony.
- Ask about mitigation: what you did to reduce damages (attempts to re-rent, notices, communications).
- If custody context is relevant, opposing counsel may probe about relevance or attempt to introduce unrelated facts — be cautious and consult Maria.

Documents and exhibits to have organized and ready
- Lease or rental agreement (signed pages).
- Rent ledger showing payments and balances (bank statements, canceled checks, Venmo/PayPal records).
- All notices served (3-day pay/quit, 30/60-day notices) and proof of service (signed receipts, certified mail, process server affidavit).
- Written communications: emails, texts, letters, messages showing demands, negotiations, notices, or admissions.
- Photos and videos showing condition of premises (with timestamps if possible).
- Repair invoices, receipts, contractor estimates, bids.
- Police reports, inspection reports, code violation notices (if applicable).
- Witness statements or declarations (roommates, neighbors, property manager).
- Discovery responses and any deposition excerpts that were useful.
- Accounting/calculation sheet explaining how you calculated the amount sought.
- Any prior court orders or related case numbers (including custody case if it’s relevant and admissible).
Bring multiple tabbed sets: one for the judge, one for opposing counsel, and one for your own use.

How to organize your exhibits
- Create an exhibit list (Exhibit A, B, C...) with a one-line description and date.
- Mark physical exhibits and keep originals in a separate folder; provide copies for the judge and opposition.
- Prepare a short timeline one-pager (dates, actions, key documents) to hand to the judge — judges appreciate concise timelines.

Witness preparation
- Meet witnesses in advance; review facts only (no coaching on false testimony).
- Tell them to keep answers short and to the point; identify documents they can authenticate.
- Prepare witnesses for cross-examination questions they might face and remind them to stick to firsthand facts.
- If a witness is hostile, discuss impeachment exhibits with Maria ahead of time.

How to handle testimony in court
- Listen to the question fully before answering.
- Keep answers short and factual — don’t volunteer extra information.
- If you don’t know or don’t remember, say that plainly instead of guessing.
- If opposing counsel asks badgering or irrelevant questions, pause and ask Maria whether to object or ask for clarification.
- Be professional and calm; demeanor affects credibility.

Other practical items to confirm with Maria
- Any local San Diego County small claims practices (judge preferences, how exhibits should be filed).
- Whether to file an updated exhibit list or any pre-hearing brief.
- Whether any evidence needs authentication or expert witness affidavits.
- The best settlement negotiation strategy and bottom-line settlement threshold.

Sample short email you can send Maria (if you want a template)
- “Maria — discovery is complete and I’m preparing for the hearing. Can we cover: expected timeline to hearing, which exhibits I must bring (lease, rent ledger, notices), whether you will appear, likely judge/opposing-counsel questions, and a recommended settlement range? I can bring a timeline and exhibit copies to our meeting. Available times: [insert].”

Final checklist (quick)
- Confirm court and hearing format (in-person vs remote).
- Ask Maria the specific pretrial deadlines.
- Prepare exhibit sets and exhibit list.
- Create a one-page timeline.
- Prepare witness declarations and inform witnesses.
- Run through anticipated judge and opposing counsel questions with Maria (practice answers).
- Discuss settlement options and post-judgment enforcement.

If you want, I can:
- Draft the short email template to Maria with the key questions above.
- Help you build an exhibit list or a one-page timeline if you paste dates/documents.
- Produce a short script of concise answers to the judge’s likely questions to rehearse.

Which of those would you like next?

