I can help you get organized and prepare the right questions to ask Maria Santos so you’ll be ready for the next steps. Below I’ve put together (A) a short list of clarifying questions for me, (B) a set of practical questions to ask your attorney, (C) a checklist of documents and evidence to have ready, (D) likely questions a judge or opposing counsel may ask and how to prepare brief answers, and (E) pre-hearing tasks/next-step items to confirm with your lawyer.

A — Quick clarifying questions for you (so I can tailor this further)
- Is the case actually in small claims court for a monetary claim (security deposit, unpaid rent, damages) or is this an unlawful detainer/eviction action in superior court? (Those are different procedures.)
- Who exactly is the opposing party in this matter (the tenant, co‑owner, other parent in a custody matter you mentioned)? Are they represented by counsel?
- Do you already have a hearing date or any pre-hearing deadlines from the court?
- Any safety or custody issues that might affect appearing in court or bringing certain witnesses?

B — Questions to ask Maria Santos (to cover procedure, evidence, strategy, and logistics)
- Court and claim specifics
  - Which court and case type is this (small claims vs. unlawful detainer vs. civil)? Are there any local San Diego rules I must follow?
  - What is the current timeline and when will the hearing be scheduled?
  - Are there any outstanding pre-hearing filings or deadlines I need to meet (exhibit lists, witness lists, declarations)?
- Evidence and exhibits
  - What documents and exhibits should I bring and how many copies does the court require?
  - Are any documents we expect to use vulnerable to exclusion (hearsay or authentication issues)? If so, how should we authenticate them?
  - Do you recommend submitting witness declarations or subpoenaing witnesses? Who should we call to testify?
- Likely lines of questioning and strategy
  - What questions do you expect the judge to ask me? What questions do you expect opposing counsel to ask?
  - What are our strongest facts and our biggest weaknesses? How should I address those weaknesses in my testimony?
  - What is our theory of the case and what is the concise factual story I should stick to when testifying?
- Damages and remedies
  - Exactly how should we calculate damages (rent owed, repairs, deposit, fees, interest)? What supporting documents do you want?
  - If counterclaims are possible, what do we need to prepare to defend against them?
- Settlement and negotiation
  - Do you recommend mediation or settlement discussions before the hearing? What is my acceptable settlement range?
  - If the other party offers something at the hearing, what authority do you need from me to settle on the spot?
- Logistics and courtroom procedure
  - How long do you expect the hearing to last? Should I prepare a short opening statement?
  - What is the courtroom etiquette and do you recommend I bring a binder with marked exhibits and a timeline?
  - Will you be present at the hearing and, if not, what should I expect?
- Costs and follow-up
  - What are the likely attorney fees and costs moving forward? How are fees handled if we win?
  - If we win, how is collection/enforcement handled? What steps do we need to prepare in case of an award?

C — Evidence & documents checklist (bring originals and multiple copies)
- Lease/rental agreement or written tenancy terms
- Rent ledger/accounting showing payments and arrears (bank statements, canceled checks)
- All notices served/received (3-day pay or quit, 30/60-day notices), and proof of service
- Move-in and move-out condition reports; key receipts/hand-over records
- Security deposit receipts and itemized deductions (if deposit dispute)
- Photos/videos of property condition (dated) and any repairs needed
- Repair invoices, contractor estimates, receipts and timelines of repairs
- Texts/emails/letters between you and the opposing party about rent, repairs, or termination
- Police reports, code enforcement complaints, or municipal records if applicable
- Discovery responses and all documents exchanged in discovery
- Witness contact info and short written statements or declarations (if allowed)
- Any court orders, pleadings, prior judgments, or related custody case orders that may be relevant
- A one-page timeline of events (dates + short facts) for quick reference
Bring at least three copies of each key document (judge, opposing counsel, your copy) unless local rules say otherwise.

D — Typical judge and opposing counsel questions and how to prepare concise answers
(Practice short factual answers; avoid speculation. Stick to dates, amounts, and actions.)

Possible judge questions
- “What is your claim and what remedy are you asking for?” — Prepare a one-sentence summary and dollar amount.
- “When did the tenancy start and end?” — Know exact dates (or best estimate).
- “Was there a written lease? What did it say about rent and deposits?” — Bring the lease and point to the clauses.
- “Were proper notices given?” — Have dates and copies of notices and proof of service.
- “What efforts did you take to mitigate damages (re-rent, repairs)?” — Show ads, communications, or dates when you offered re-rental.
- “How did you calculate the amount you’re claiming?” — Bring your rent ledger/calculation with supporting receipts.
- “Did you return or account for the security deposit?” — Provide deposit receipt and itemized deductions.

Possible opposing counsel questions (cross-exam style)
- Questions meant to undermine credibility or show inconsistencies:
  - “Isn’t it true you did X on Y date?” — Be truthful; if unsure, say you don’t recall but give proximate facts and offer to check documents.
  - “Why didn’t you accept partial rent or try to re-rent sooner?” — Explain actions and reasons succinctly (back with evidence when possible).
  - “Did you ever discuss this with the other parent/caretaker?” — State facts and point to communications.
- Questions about repairs or habitability:
  - “When were repairs requested and by whom? How long did it take you to respond?” — Have timestamps on requests and repair invoices.
- Questions about money:
  - “Show me proof of the amounts you claim.” — Have receipts, ledger, and bank records.

Practice: ask Maria to do a short mock examination/cross so you can rehearse concise answers and avoid volunteering extraneous information.

E — Pre-hearing tasks and items to confirm with your lawyer
- Confirm exact court, date/time, courtroom, and parking/security rules.
- Prepare an exhibit list and pre-mark exhibits if required by the court; get the required number of copies.
- Decide on witnesses and get signed declarations or subpoena them if needed.
- Prepare a one-page factual timeline and a one-sentence case summary to say at the start.
- Discuss likely settlement range and whether mediation should be scheduled now.
- Ask Maria to identify any discovery follow-ups or unresolved discovery issues that might affect admissibility.
- Confirm plan for enforcement of judgment and next steps if we win (e.g., wage garnishment, levy).

If you’d like, I can:
- Turn these into a printable checklist or email draft to send to Maria (I can draft the email for you), or
- Draft a short script you can practice responding to the judge’s likely questions, or
- Help you create the one-page timeline and exhibit list if you give me the dates/documents.

Tell me which of those you want next (draft email to Maria, practice script, timeline/exhibit list), and answer the clarifying questions in section A so I can tailor it precisely.

