Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — I can help you get organized and ready. Below is a practical, ready-to-use checklist of things to ask your attorney (Maria Santos) and the likely questions you should prepare to answer from the judge or opposing counsel. I also include a documents/exhibits checklist and tips for how to present answers in court. If you want, I can draft an email to Maria with these questions for you to send or save as a draft.

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
- Case status & next steps
  - Where exactly are we in the process now, and what are the next court deadlines (hearing date, pretrial conference, filing deadlines)?
  - Will you appear with me or only provide coaching? (Note: in California small claims court, attorneys normally cannot represent clients in the courtroom — please confirm how you will participate.)
  - What is our overall goal for the hearing (money judgment, possession, damages, set-off, costs)?

- Evidence & documentation
  - Do we have all the evidence the court will expect to see to prove my claims/defenses? What am I missing?
  - Which documents should I bring to the hearing, and in what order should I present them?
  - How many copies of each exhibit should I bring (for judge, opposing party, myself)?
  - Are there issues with admissibility (hearsay, business records) I should be aware of, and how do we authenticate documents?

- Notices & procedure
  - Were all required notices properly served (3-day pay/quit, 30-day/60-day, notice to terminate) and do we have proof of service?
  - Did the opposing party follow the eviction procedure correctly? If not, how should I emphasize that at hearing?
  - Are there local San Diego County or California tenant protections or ordinances (e.g., AB 1482 or local ordinances) that apply and could affect the outcome?

- Claims, damages, and calculations
  - Exactly what damages will I ask the court to award (unpaid rent, late fees, repairs, cleaning, unlawful holdover rent, statutory fees, court costs)?
  - How should I compute damages and present the math clearly to the judge?
  - If the opposing party raises counterclaims (e.g., habitability, security deposit, damage claims), how should I respond?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Who should testify on my behalf (property manager, witness to service, repair contractor, neighbor)? How should witnesses be prepared?
  - Are written witness statements acceptable, and how do we introduce them?

- Strategy & likely arguments
  - What are the strongest points for our case and the weakest points we need to shore up?
  - What defenses or tactics do you expect opposing counsel/party to raise, and how should I respond?
  - Should we try mediation or settlement before the hearing? What settlement range is reasonable?

- Practical courtroom questions
  - What is the hearing format and time limit for presenting my case?
  - How should I mark and present exhibits? (Labeling, copies, binder order)
  - What behavior and phrasing will make the best impression on the judge (tone, brevity, addressing the judge)?

- Post-judgment & enforcement
  - If we win, how do we enforce the judgment (writ of execution, garnishment, bank levy, eviction enforcement)?
  - If we lose, what are the appeal or post-judgment options and deadlines?
  - Who handles collections and enforcement — you or do I need to take separate steps?

Documents and exhibits to bring (checklist)
- Lease or rental agreement (signed, including any amendments)
- Notice(s) served (e.g., 3-day, 30/60-day, cure/quit) and proof of service (signed proof of service or certified mail receipts)
- Rent ledger or accounting showing payments, credits, and outstanding balance (clear arithmetic)
- Copies of checks, bank records, or electronic payment records showing payments and dates
- Written communications: emails, texts, written notices between parties
- Photographs or video (date-stamped if possible) showing property condition or damages
- Repair invoices, estimates, receipts, contractor statements
- Inspection reports, municipal notices, code violations, or habitability reports (if relevant)
- Witness list and brief written statements or contact info
- Any prior court orders, small claims forms, or related case documents
- ID and copies for yourself, and at least two copies of each exhibit (judge, opposing party, you)

How to organize exhibits
- Number each exhibit (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2, etc.). Put tabs and an exhibit list (index) in front.
- Put one exhibit copy in a simple binder or folder in the order you plan to present them.
- Bring at least two sets of copies (one for the judge, one for the opposing party) and keep the originals with you.

Questions the judge is likely to ask (prepare short factual answers)
- What is your relationship to the property/what is this case about? (One-sentence summary.)
- When did the tenancy begin? What is the monthly rent? What did the tenant do/who breached?
- Were proper notices given? How and when were they served? (Point to proof of service.)
- How much is owed (show rent ledger/exhibit and simple calculation)?
- What are you asking the court to order (possession, $X damages, costs)?
- Did you try to resolve this before filing? (Show communications/offer to cure, if any.)
- If the tenant claims habitability issues: what repairs were requested and how were they handled? (Show repair records.)
- If there are safety or custody concerns (you mentioned opposing party is the other parent): is there any safety risk or restraining order relevance? (Be factual; raise safety concerns with your attorney ahead of time.)

Questions opposing counsel or the opposing party might ask (and how to prepare)
- “Was proper notice given?” — Have proof of service and the notice itself ready.
- “Is your rent calculation correct?” — Bring a clear rent ledger and supporting payment records.
- “Did you accept rent after serving a notice?” — Be prepared to explain whether accepting rent affected the notice.
- “Did you make repairs or did you retaliate?” — Have repair records, timelines, and communications.
- “Are you harassing or trying to evict for retaliatory or discriminatory reasons?” — Keep answers factual, avoid emotional language, and show documentation that supports your reason for eviction.
- “Do you have evidence that damages exceed normal wear and tear?” — Bring contractor estimates, receipts, and dated photos.
- If custody or other family law matters could be raised: ask your attorney how those issues should be addressed in small claims and whether to keep the focus strictly on landlord/tenant facts.

How to answer questions in court (practical tips)
- Keep answers brief and factual. Start with the direct answer, then cite an exhibit (e.g., “Yes. Proof of service is Exhibit 3.”).
- Avoid arguing with the other party in open court; let the judge handle objections.
- If you don’t know an answer, say “I don’t know” and offer to provide supporting documents rather than guessing.
- Practice a 60–90 second opening statement that summarizes the case, your requested relief, and key evidence points.

Procedural points to confirm with Maria
- Time limits for testimony and presentation in that small claims location/department.
- Local San Diego County small claims rules and any courthouse-specific procedures (exhibit handling, electronic filing, camera/video policies).
- Whether any pretrial settlement conference or mediation is scheduled/available.
- Whether to file any pre-hearing declarations or witness lists.

Other important items
- If the opposing party is the other parent in a custody case, ask how (or if) those family law dynamics might affect the small claims case (safety concerns, communication pathways, or court coordination).
- Ask about preserving evidence (do not delete texts/emails), and whether any additional discovery or subpoenas for documents/witnesses would help.
- Discuss credibility: how to address and document inconsistent statements by the other party.

Offer to help further
- I can draft a concise email to Maria Santos that lists these questions and asks for specific next steps — would you like me to draft that now?
- If you want, we can also role-play likely judge or opposing counsel questions and practice concise answers.

Would you like the email to Maria drafted (and do you prefer formal or conversational tone)? If yes, tell me whether to include your case caption or any dates/figures to put into the email.

