I can help you get organized. First — one important clarification I need from you before I get more specific: is this actually a small claims case, or an unlawful‑detainer/eviction case in San Diego County Superior Court? (Small claims courts in California generally don’t handle eviction/unlawful‑detainer matters and discovery is very limited; the fact you had a discovery phase suggests this is likely in Superior Court.) Also, are you the landlord or the tenant? Who exactly is the “opposing party” in this eviction—is it the tenant who is also the other parent in a custody case? Those details change the practical questions to ask your attorney.

Below is a structured set of things to ask Maria Santos and a list of likely judge and opposing‑counsel questions, plus a checklist and prep tips so you’ll be ready for the next steps.

Questions to ask your attorney (clear, direct phrasing you can use)
- Case status & procedure
  - Can you confirm the court and calendar type (small claims vs Superior Court unlawful‑detainer)? Which courthouse and courtroom will the hearing be in?
  - What is the current timeline and anticipated hearing date? How long will the hearing/trial be scheduled for?
  - What is the hearing format (bench trial, short cause, motion hearing, settlement conference)? Who will be the decisionmaker (judge vs commissioner)?
- Evidence & exhibits
  - What specific documents and exhibits do we need to have ready and in what format? How many copies should I bring (judge, court clerk, opposing party, us)?
  - Which documents are likely to be most persuasive? Any documents that might be excluded or objected to?
  - Should we prepare an exhibit list and premark exhibits? If so, what labeling format do you prefer?
- Witnesses and testimony
  - Who do we plan to call as witnesses, and what will each witness testify to?
  - Do you recommend I prepare a witness statement or affidavit for anyone? Should we subpoena anyone?
  - How should I prepare my own testimony—what points should I emphasize or avoid volunteering?
- Anticipated legal arguments & questions
  - What defenses or arguments do you expect opposing counsel to press?
  - Are there any weaknesses in our case we need to address before the hearing?
  - What legal standards will the judge use to decide this case?
- Discovery & pretrial logistics
  - Are any pretrial filings still needed (exhibit lists, pretrial statement, trial brief, motions in limine)?
  - Were there any discovery issues that might lead to sanctions or exclude evidence? If so, how are we addressing them?
- Settlement & alternatives
  - What are realistic settlement options? Should we consider mediation or a settlement conference before the hearing?
  - If we settle, what terms should we ask for to protect my interests (e.g., payment plan, move‑out terms, release language)?
- Remedies, enforcement & costs
  - If we win, what remedies are available (possession only, monetary judgment, costs, attorneys’ fees)? How is enforcement handled?
  - If we lose, what are our options (appeal, vacating judgment, collection defenses)?
- Practical courtroom guidance
  - How should I conduct myself in court (where to sit, when to speak, how to address the judge)?
  - Do you want any signals from me during testimony or cross‑examination? How will we coordinate?
- Fees & next steps
  - What are your estimated fees and costs from here through the hearing?
  - What immediate steps should I take before the hearing (gather documents, contact witnesses, get photos, repair receipts)?

Specific documents and items you should bring/prepare
- Lease or rental agreement (signed pages)
- Rent ledger / payment history (dates & amounts)
- Notices served (3‑day pay/quit, 30/60‑day notices, termination notices) with proof of service
- Repair requests, maintenance logs, inspection reports, habitability communications
- All communications with tenant/opposing party: emails, texts, letters, voicemail summaries
- Photographs and videos, with date/time and brief captions describing what they show
- Receipts, invoices, bank records showing payments or costs related to property
- Any police reports, code enforcement reports, or third‑party documents
- Discovery responses, interrogatories, responses, production documents you received and sent
- Witness list and short summaries of what each witness will say
- Timeline of events (one‑page chronological timeline is very useful)
- Copies of prior court filings (complaint, answer, motions, discovery motions, court orders)
- Proposed orders or judgment forms (if you want the judge to sign an order at the hearing)
- Identification (bring your ID) and any subpoenas you served or plan to serve

Likely questions the judge may ask (general examples)
- For landlord/plaintiff: Why are you asking the court for possession / what is the legal basis for eviction?
- When was the rent due, and when was the last rent payment received?
- Did you serve the required notice(s)? When and how were they served?
- Have you attempted any alternatives (payment plans, repair, mediation)?
- Are the documents you submitted authentic? Can you explain any gaps or inconsistencies?
- For tenant/defendant: Why have you not paid / what defenses do you assert (repair and deduct, rent withholding, payment made)?
- If the issue involves habitability: When did you first notify the landlord and what was the response?

Likely topics opposing counsel may question you about
- Credibility and timeline (inconsistent dates, memory lapses)
- Authenticity and completeness of documents (missing pages, lack of originals)
- Motive (e.g., retaliatory eviction, improper purpose)
- Habitability or landlord obligations (repair history, notice compliance)
- Mitigation (efforts to resolve without court)
- Damages and calculation (how amounts were computed, supporting receipts)

How to answer questions in court (practical tips)
- Pause and think before answering; keep answers short and directly responsive.
- Address your answers to the judge, not to the opposing counsel.
- Don’t volunteer extra information; if you don’t remember, say so honestly.
- If you need to refer to a document, have it marked and refer to the exhibit number.
- Maintain professional demeanor even if provoked.

Checklist for the days before the hearing
- Meet with Maria for a focused prep session and mock Q&A.
- Finalize and premark exhibits; assemble exhibit binder and copies for the court and opposing counsel.
- Confirm witness availability and subpoenas if needed.
- Prepare a 1–page timeline and a 1–page “key points” summary for Maria to use in opening/closing.
- Review any local court rules and the judge’s courtroom preferences (ask Maria to confirm).
- Confirm logistics (arrival time, parking, security rules, check‑in at clerk’s window).

Questions to ask Maria in your meeting to maximize prep time (concise list)
- What are the three strongest points in our case, and the three weakest?
- What documents or testimony would most likely change the outcome?
- How should I behave when testifying? What not to say?
- Do you want me to prepare a sworn declaration summarizing events?
- Can we run a mock direct/cross examination now?

Would you like me to:
- Draft an email to Maria summarizing these questions and asking for a meeting?
- Create a printable one‑page timeline template and exhibit checklist you can fill in?
- Help you tailor the questions and checklist if you tell me whether you’re the landlord or tenant and confirm court type (small claims vs Superior Court)?

Note: I’m not your attorney and this isn’t legal advice. Use these items to guide your discussion with Maria Santos, who will provide legal strategy specific to your case.

