I can help — this is a good time to get organized. A few quick clarifying questions before I lay out a detailed checklist and suggested questions to ask Maria Santos:

1. Is this actually in small claims court, or is it an unlawful detainer (eviction) case in San Diego County Superior Court? (Evictions are usually handled in Superior Court; small claims is for money-only disputes.)
2. Do you already have a hearing date, and will Maria be the attorney of record at the hearing? Or will you be representing yourself at the hearing with Maria advising?
3. Is the claim only for possession (eviction), or are damages/money (e.g., unpaid rent, property damage) part of this proceeding?
4. Would you like me to draft a concise email to Maria listing these questions and asking for next steps?

Once you confirm (or if you prefer to skip those clarifications), here’s a practical, organized list you can use right away — questions to ask Maria, likely lines of questioning from the judge or opposing counsel, documents to have ready, witness prep, and practical steps.

Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Court & process
  - Which court and case type is this (small claims vs. Superior Court unlawful detainer)? What are the procedural differences we should expect?
  - What is the current timeline (hearing date, deadlines, possible continuances)?
  - Will you attend/represent me at the hearing? If not, what will your role be?
- Case strategy & outcome expectations
  - What is our legal theory and requested relief (possession only, rent, damages, fees)?
  - What are our strongest facts and our case’s weak points?
  - What outcomes are realistic (possession, monetary judgment, costs/attorney fees)?
  - What is the settlement posture and the range at which we should consider settling?
- Evidence & admissibility
  - Which specific documents will the judge expect to see and which could be excluded as hearsay?
  - Do any documents need authentication, affidavits, or witness testimony to be admissible?
  - Should we prepare a trial brief, exhibit list, or proposed order?
- Notices & service
  - Do we have proper proof of service for every notice (3-day, 30/60-day, unlawful detainer summons, etc.)? Are there any service defects that the other side could use?
- Witnesses & subpoenas
  - Who should testify for us? Should we subpoena any witnesses or records (e.g., bank records, phone records)?
  - Will you lead the direct examination or should I? If I testify, how should I prepare and what should I avoid saying?
- Cross-examination & sensitive issues
  - What lines of cross-examination do you expect from opposing counsel (including any questions about the custody case or personal matters)?
  - How should I respond to questions about motive, credibility, or unrelated personal matters?
- Remedies & enforcement
  - If we win, what’s the enforcement process (eviction/possession timeline, collecting money judgments)?
  - What are appeal rights and deadlines?
- Costs & fees
  - What will the hearing cost (additional fees, time, additional discovery, subpoenas)? Are attorney fees recoverable?
- Logistics
  - Will the hearing be in-person or remote? What are courtroom procedures and local judge preferences in San Diego (exhibit format, number of copies, electronic submission)?
  - How far in advance should we be at court, and what ID/documents should I bring?

Likely questions the judge or opposing counsel will ask (tailored for eviction/unlawful detainer + money claims)
- Establishing tenancy and possession
  - Are you the landlord/tenant? When did the tenancy begin? Is there a written lease? What is the rent amount and payment schedule?
  - Did the tenant receive proper written notice to pay or quit / terminate tenancy? When and how was it served? Did they receive it?
- Amounts due and damages
  - What is the total amount you claim? Show the rent ledger, receipts, invoices, and calculations.
  - How did you calculate damages or repair costs? Show estimates and receipts.
- Timeline & events
  - What sequence of events led to filing? (Dates of notices, nonpayment, failure to vacate, repairs, communications.)
- Communications & attempts to resolve
  - What attempts did you make to collect rent or resolve the issue? Show texts/emails/calls.
- Credibility & relationships
  - Is there any relationship between parties (co-parents, family)? Could there be bias, motive to delay/retaliate?
  - Have there been prior lawsuits or other court orders between the parties?
- Habitability/defense issues (if tenant raises them)
  - Were repair requests made? Were repairs performed? Did habitability or safety factor into withholding rent or other defenses?

Documents and evidence checklist (bring originals and several copies)
- Lease or rental agreement (signed)
- Notices: 3-day pay/quit, 30/60/90-day termination, and proof of service (signed proof)
- Unlawful detainer complaint and summons (if filed)
- Rent ledger showing amounts due, payments, dates, late fees
- Bank statements, canceled checks, payment receipts, or money-order receipts
- All communications: emails, text message logs, letters (print with dates and phone numbers)
- Photos or videos (dated or with metadata if possible) of property condition or damages
- Repair invoices, estimates, contractor receipts, receipts for supplies
- Police reports or incident reports (if applicable)
- Witness names, contact info, and short statements/affidavits
- Discovery responses and produced documents
- Any prior court orders or related case dockets (custody case if relevant)
- Proposed exhibit list and multiple exhibit sets (one for judge, one for opposing counsel, one for your file)
- ID and any required court forms

Witness handling and preparation
- Decide who will testify and why (tenant, property manager, contractor, neighbor).
- Have short written witness statements signed and dated.
- Prepare witnesses with a mock direct and cross-examination: keep answers short, factual, and anchored to documents.
- Subpoena witnesses or records early if they may not come voluntarily.

Courtroom strategy and testimony tips
- Be concise, calm, factual. Answer the question asked — don’t volunteer extra information.
- If you don’t remember, say so. Don’t guess. (“I don’t recall” is acceptable.)
- Practice a 1–2 minute opening summary that states: what you’re asking the court to do, key facts, and the evidence you will present.
- Label exhibits clearly, bring extra copies, and know the chain of custody/authentication for each item.
- Avoid getting drawn into unrelated personal disputes (e.g., custody issues) unless they’re directly relevant and your attorney advises otherwise.
- Dress professionally, arrive early, and follow local courtroom rules.

Possible defenses/lines opposing counsel may use
- Deny proper notice or defective service
- Argue habitability or repair issues justify nonpayment
- Claim misunderstanding, partial payment, or withholding for legitimate reasons
- Attack credibility by pointing to inconsistencies or unrelated personal matters (custody history, prior disputes)
- Argue our damages are speculative or unsupported

Practical next steps (action checklist)
- Confirm which court and hearing date; verify Maria’s role at hearing.
- Ask Maria to review and pre-mark exhibits and prepare an exhibit binder.
- Produce a short exhibit index and timeline for Maria.
- Schedule a mock Q&A or witness prep session with Maria before the hearing.
- Confirm any necessary subpoenas and local rules for exhibit submission in San Diego County.
- Determine settlement bottom line and what offers we will accept/reject.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a concise email to Maria listing the above questions and asking the clarifying questions (I can make it short and focused), or
- Create a one-page exhibit checklist/timeline you can bring to court, or
- Draft a mock Q&A for practicing your testimony?

Tell me which one and provide any missing details (hearing date, whether Maria will attend, and court type) and I’ll draft the email or documents for you.

