Below is a focused checklist you can use when you talk with your attorney and to prepare for the hearing. I’ve grouped items so it’s easy to bring them up in a meeting or an email.

1) Confirm case basics with your attorney
- Which court and department (Small Claims vs. Superior Court / unlawful detainer)? Confirm hearing type (trial/hearing/status conference) and date window.
- What is the exact legal claim(s) and potential remedies the opposing party is seeking and what we are seeking (damages, possession, rent, fees)?
- Are there any local San Diego County courtroom rules or procedures we need to follow (number of copies, filing deadlines, remote appearance, exhibit labeling)?

2) Procedural questions to ask Maria
- Has discovery truly closed? Are there any outstanding discovery or motion deadlines we should expect?
- Are there any pending motions (motions in limine, summary judgment, sanctions) we should file or expect?
- What is the likely timeline from hearing to judgment and from judgment to collection/enforcement?
- If this is small claims: can you represent me in court or only advise? (In California, attorneys generally cannot appear for parties in small claims court—confirm practical implications.)
- Do we need to subpoena witnesses or documents before the hearing? If so, who will handle that?

3) Evidence and document checklist to confirm with counsel
Ask your attorney whether these documents are sufficient and if anything else is needed:
- Lease/rental agreement, addenda, and any written amendments
- Notices served (pay-or-quit, cure/quit, termination notices) with proof of service
- Rent ledger and receipts, bank records showing payments/partial payments/returned checks
- Written communications: emails, texts, letters, messaging app screenshots (date-stamped)
- Repair requests, work orders, invoices, receipts, contractor estimates
- Photographs and videos showing property condition (with dates), inspection reports
- Code enforcement or housing department complaints and responses
- Police reports or incident reports if applicable
- Any prior court orders, disposition or judgments involving the property or parties
- Witness statements / contact info for tenants, neighbors, contractors
- Damages/supporting calculations and back-up (how you computed amounts)
- Any settlement offers or demand letters exchanged

4) Questions to ask about likely lines of questioning (judge and opposing counsel)
General:
- What factual timeline do you want me to stick to? (event-by-event concise statement)
- What are the critical facts that must be established to win/defend?
From the judge:
- Expect questions about the timeline (dates of notice, breaches, payments, communications).
- Expect questions about service of notices (how, when, proof).
- Expect questions about mitigation: what did you do to avoid/limit the dispute (attempts to collect, repair, offer alternatives)?
- If custody or parenting issues are connected: expect questions about relevance — why this other parent is involved and whether allegations overlap with custody matters.
From opposing counsel:
- Expect attempts to challenge your credibility or memory, highlight inconsistencies, or present alternate versions of events.
- Expect detailed questioning about payments, receipts, and dates — be prepared to show exact records.
- Expect questions designed to expand scope (bringing in unrelated incidents) — ask counsel when to object or ask for a sidebar.

5) Cross-examination preparation
- Practice short, factual answers. Avoid volunteering extra information.
- If you don’t remember, say “I don’t recall,” rather than guessing.
- If a document contradicts your memory, acknowledge it and explain the discrepancy briefly.
- Know your timeline cold (dates and actions).
- Work with Maria on common traps: leading questions, compound questions, and hypothetical questions.

6) Evidence/admissibility issues to clear with your attorney
- How will exhibits be authenticated? Do we need witness foundation testimony for each item (who created/received it)?
- Any hearsay concerns and whether exceptions apply (business records, admissions, prior statements).
- Are there documents we should file or identify in advance to avoid surprises?
- For photos/videos: ensure metadata is retained or witness can testify to the date/time.

7) Witness planning
- Who will testify for us? Who will the opposing party call?
- Which witnesses need subpoenas for attendance or documents?
- Do any witnesses need prep sessions (what to expect, how to answer)?
- Are there expert witnesses needed (e.g., for property condition or repair cost)?

8) Settlement and negotiation
- What is our settlement authority and target range? At what point do we accept/reject?
- Are structured settlements or payment plans options?
- If settlement is proposed at the hearing, what are the procedure and safeguards (written agreement, dismissal paperwork)?

9) Remedies, fees, and post-judgment issues
- What damages and remedies can we realistically obtain (rent, damages, attorneys’ fees, costs)?
- What are the costs of enforcing a judgment in San Diego County (writ of execution, garnishment)?
- Appeal or post-judgment motions: timelines and likelihood.

10) Practical hearing logistics (ask attorney to confirm)
- How long will our presentation likely be? Who will speak first?
- How many copies of exhibits to bring (judge, opposing party, court file) and any specific label format?
- Will the hearing be in-person or remote? If remote, what platform and rules for exhibits?
- Courtroom etiquette and how to address the judge.
- If you’re representing yourself at the hearing, what parts will Maria handle (preparation, documents, strategy)?

11) Specific questions you should ask Maria in your meeting/email (copy-paste)
- Please confirm which court this is (Small Claims vs Superior Court/unlawful detainer) and the department/room.
- What specific questions should I expect the judge to ask me at the hearing?
- What lines of questioning should I expect from opposing counsel?
- Which documents/exhibits do you consider essential and do we need originals or certified copies?
- Who will testify? Do we need subpoenas? Can you draft/serve them?
- Are there any motions we should file before the hearing?
- What is our best and worst-case outcome and a realistic settlement range?
- Do you recommend any additional evidence or witnesses we don’t already have?
- If the opposing party raises issues from the custody case, how should I respond?
- Do you want me to prepare and bring a one-page timeline and exhibit list for the judge?

12) Quick checklist to bring to the meeting and to the hearing
- Folder with organized tabs: timeline (one-page), exhibits (numbered), witness contact list, copies of all documents (one for judge, one for opposing party, one for you), extra pens, laptop/flash drive with photos, proof of service docs, subpoena copies (if issued).
- Written short script (2-3 sentences) of your opening statement and succinct answers to anticipated questions.

13) Communication and next steps
- Ask Maria for a short prep session to rehearse answers and review exhibits (30–60 minutes).
- Request a prioritized checklist of 3–5 things you must do before the hearing (e.g., get receipts, contact witness, produce exhibits).
- Ask whether she wants to draft and file a hearing brief or witness list.

