I can help you get organized. A few quick clarifying questions before I go into detailed checklists and sample questions:
- Are you the landlord or the tenant in this eviction matter?
- Is the case actually set in small claims court, or is it an unlawful detainer/action in Superior Court? (Eviction procedures and limits differ by court.)
- Do you already have a hearing date, or is that still pending?

Below is a focused, practical guide you can use at your next meeting with Maria Santos. I’ve separated (A) what to ask your attorney, (B) the kinds of questions a judge might ask, (C) questions opposing counsel may ask in discovery/hearing, (D) documents and evidence to prepare, and (E) trial/hearing prep and logistics.

A. Questions to ask your attorney (use these at your next meeting)
- Case status & timeline
  - What is the exact calendar date/time/location of the hearing (or when will it be set)?
  - What are the next procedural deadlines (pretrial filings, exhibit lists, witness lists, motions)?
  - Are there any motions we should file now (e.g., motion in limine, summary judgment, sanctions)?
- Jurisdiction & remedy limits
  - Is this in small claims or Superior Court? What monetary/jurisdictional limits apply?
  - What remedies can the court grant (possession, money damages, attorney fees, costs, etc.)?
- Case theory & strategy
  - What is our primary legal theory and possible alternative theories? (E.g., unlawful detainer defenses, breach of lease, habitability, wrongful eviction, rent withholding, retaliatory eviction.)
  - What is our settlement posture? Are there settlement ranges you recommend before the hearing?
- Evidence & exhibits
  - Which documents are essential exhibits? Do we need originals or are copies acceptable?
  - How should exhibits be numbered/organized and how many copies do we bring to court?
  - Any documents we should authenticate or have witnesses verify?
- Witnesses
  - Which witnesses should testify and in what order? Do we need subpoenas for any witnesses?
  - What will each witness be asked and what answers do we expect?
  - Any expert witnesses needed (repair estimates, forensic accounting, parenting/custody relevance if applicable)?
- Cross-examination & weaknesses
  - What weaknesses do you see in our case and how should I handle them on the stand?
  - What admissions or statements should I avoid making?
- Discovery & procedural issues post-discovery
  - Are there any outstanding discovery issues to follow up on (supplemental responses, motions to compel, sanctions)?
  - Should we file any pre-hearing statements or briefs?
- Logistics & courtroom conduct
  - How long will I be allowed to speak/testify? Will there be strict time limits?
  - How should I dress and conduct myself? When should I arrive?
  - Will you be present and represent me at the hearing? Who will do direct/cross?
- Practical outcomes & enforcement
  - If we win, how do we enforce the judgment (eviction order, collection procedures)?
  - If we lose or get an unfavorable judgment, what are our appeal or post-judgment options?

B. Questions the judge is likely to ask
(Practice concise, truthful answers for these.)
- What do you want the court to order? (Be specific: possession, rent arrears, damages, etc.)
- What is the timeline of key events? (Lease start date, missed rent dates, notices given, move-out date, repairs, communications.)
- Did you provide the required notices (e.g., notice to pay or quit, 3/30-day notice, notice to cure)? When and how were they served?
- Has the opposing party/tenant paid rent? What is owed (provide calculation)?
- Have you attempted to mitigate damages or resolve the issue by settlement?
- Are there any repairs/habitability issues and what did you do in response?
- Are you seeking attorney’s fees or costs and under what basis?
- Are there any outstanding discovery disputes or pending motions?
- Is the evidence you’re offering admissible/authenticated?

C. Questions opposing counsel (or opposing party) is likely to ask you
(These are typical cross-examination aims: test credibility, show inconsistencies, pin down facts.)
- When exactly did the tenancy start and how long did the tenant/landlord occupy the property?
- What did you tell the tenant and when (show emails, texts, letters)?
- How did you serve the notice(s)? Who received them?
- Did you accept partial rent payments or make any payment arrangements?
- Have you made repairs or addressed complaints? What receipts or invoices show that?
- Do you have proof of damages and how did you calculate them?
- Have you ever been late on obligations or acted in a way that could justify the other side’s defenses (retaliation, self-help eviction)?
- Are you aware of any statements you made earlier that contradict your current claims?
- What is your relationship to witnesses and are they biased?

D. Documents and evidence checklist (bring originals if possible; have organized copies)
- Lease or rental agreement (all pages and amendments)
- Payment records: rent ledger, bank records, cancelled checks, receipts
- Notices served: notice to pay/quit, notice to vacate, cure notices (with proof of service)
- Communication logs: emails, texts, letters, voicemail transcripts (keep dates/times)
- Photographs and videos (dated) showing property condition, damage, repairs
- Repair invoices, contractor estimates, receipts
- Move-in/move-out inspection reports, inventory lists
- Police reports, code enforcement reports, or inspection reports if applicable
- Discovery responses and deposition transcripts (if any)
- Court filings: complaint, answer, motions, settlement offers, discovery responses
- Witness contact information and short witness statements/summary of expected testimony
- Damages calculation worksheet (clear math showing how you arrived at totals)
- Any prior judgments or relevant court orders (including custody-related orders if they affect occupancy)

E. Hearing prep, witness prep and courtroom tips
- Prepare a concise timeline (one page) you can hand to your attorney and the court describing key dates and actions.
- Prepare short direct-examination questions for each witness and practice them out loud. Keep answers factual and concise.
- Prep for cross-exam: practice answering leading and tough questions calmly; do not volunteer extra information.
- Authenticate documents: label exhibits, and have someone (you or a witness) briefly state how the document is genuine (e.g., “this is my lease; I signed it on X”).
- Know your numbers: rent amounts, dates, totals owed — the judge will expect precise figures.
- Bring multiple sets of exhibits: one for judge, one for opposing counsel, one for your file, and one for witness if needed.
- Dress conservatively and arrive early. Bring a binder with tabs and a notebook for notes during hearing.
- Consider a pre-hearing meeting with your attorney to rehearse direct and cross and to role-play likely questions.
- Keep emotion under control. Judges respond best to calm, factual presentation.

F. Specifics related to custody/other-party issues (if relevant)
- If the opposing party is also involved in a custody case and that affects occupancy, ask Maria how those orders interact with eviction proceedings and whether custody orders create any defenses or evidentiary complications.
- Ask whether any juvenile or family-court records are relevant or need to be protected (sealed) or excluded.

G. Suggested phrasing to use with your lawyer
- “Can you walk me through exactly what you expect the judge to ask me at the hearing so I can practice concise answers?”
- “Which documents should we prioritize as exhibits and who will authenticate them?”
- “Are there any weak points you see that I should explain in advance or prepare to justify?”
- “If opposing counsel calls X witness or offers Y document, how should I respond?”
- “What are our best settlement terms now compared to what might happen at hearing?”

Final practical checklist for your next meeting with Maria
- Bring all physical documents and a one-page timeline.
- Ask the attorney the specific questions from section A.
- Schedule a short mock testimony session to practice answering judge/cross questions.
- Confirm who will appear at the hearing and logistics (arrival time, courtroom number).

I’m not your lawyer—this is general preparation help. Maria Santos will give you advice tailored to your specific facts and jurisdiction. If you want, I can draft a one-page timeline template or a checklist email you can send to Maria asking these questions. Which would you prefer?

