This is a good moment to get organized — below is a focused, practical checklist you can use with Maria Santos. It’s divided so you can (A) confirm the case posture, (B) ask specific procedural and strategy questions of your attorney, (C) prepare for the kinds of questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask, and (D) gather/organize evidence and witnesses. At the end I include hearing-day tips and an offer to help draft an email to your attorney or to role-play Q&A.

First — a quick clarification to confirm with your lawyer (or tell me so I can tailor further):
- Is this actually a small claims case or an unlawful detainer / eviction in Superior Court? (California small claims has strict rules about attorney participation; most eviction matters are in Superior Court.)
- Are you the plaintiff (landlord) or the defendant (tenant)?
- Exactly what relief are we seeking (possession only, money for back rent, damages, fees, or something else)?
- Who is the opposing party in this case (you said “the other parent in the custody case” — are they the tenant/opposing party in this eviction)?

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
1. Case posture & strategy
- What is our current procedural posture after discovery? Any pending motions or deadlines we should know about?
- Are we proceeding to hearing or expecting settlement/mediation first?
- What is our legal theory and the key facts we must prove?
- What relief (specific orders) should we ask for at the hearing?
- What are the strengths and weaknesses of our case and of the opponent’s case?

2. Court rules & logistics (San Diego-specific)
- Which court and courtroom is the hearing in, and what are the local rules (time limits, exhibit exchange deadlines, number of witnesses, required copies)?
- How long will the hearing be scheduled for, and how much time can I expect to speak?
- Will you (Maria Santos) attend and represent me in court? If not, what will you do to prepare me for self-representation?
- Are there any forms or filings I must submit before the hearing (exhibit list, witness list, pretrial statement)?

3. Evidence & admissibility
- Which documents are most important to submit as exhibits?
- How should documents be organized and marked for court (exhibit numbers, copies for judge/opposing party)?
- Do we need sworn declarations or witness affidavits to preserve testimony?
- Any hearsay or foundation issues we need to address for our key documents (emails, texts, receipts, photos)?
- If evidence is electronic (text messages, emails), do we need metadata or printouts with timestamps?

4. Witnesses & subpoenas
- Who should testify for us? (You, witnesses, expert, property manager)
- How should witness testimony be prepared (declaration vs live testimony)?
- Do we need subpoenas for witnesses or documents? If so, when must they be served?
- If a witness is hostile, how do we handle cross-examination or impeachment?

5. Questions for trial preparation and testimony
- How should I answer the judge’s questions (tone, brevity, when to defer to counsel)?
- What are the likely lines of cross-examination from opposing counsel and how should I respond?
- What admissions would hurt our case that I must avoid or clarify?

6. Settlement & negotiation
- What is our settlement range and what are non-negotiables?
- Should we propose mediation first? What are costs and timing?
- If settlement is reached, what form should the agreement take to avoid future disputes (written stipulation, court order)?

7. Post-hearing remedies & enforcement
- If we win, how is judgment enforced (eviction/personal judgment, writ of possession, collection processes)?
- What are the likely timelines and costs for enforcement in San Diego County?
- If we lose or get an unfavorable order, what are our appeal options and timelines?

Likely judge questions (prepare concise answers with dates and documents)
- What relief are you asking for? (Be specific: possession, rent owed $X, damages $Y)
- When did the tenancy begin and when did it end (if applicable)?
- What is the rental amount and which payments are outstanding? (Have a rent ledger with dates and amounts.)
- When and how did you serve any required notices (3-day, 30/60-day, cure notices)? Bring copies with service proof.
- Did you attempt to resolve this before filing? (Show communications, demand letters, receipts.)
- If defendant: why did you withhold rent or stay after notice? (Have repair requests, receipts, or other supporting docs.)
- Are there any safety, habitability, or code issues? (Bring inspection reports, photos, police reports.)
- Have you accepted partial payments or had any payment agreements? (Bring bank records, text/email confirmations.)
- If possession is sought: is anyone left in the property? Are there children or pets? (Be factual.)

Likely opposing counsel (or opposing party) questions to prepare for
- Questions that test your timeline: “When did X occur?” “When did you provide notice?” “Can you show me that?”
- Questions aimed at credibility: inconsistencies with prior statements/documents, gaps in records.
- Questions testing mitigation: “What efforts did you make to reduce damages?” “Did you offer alternatives?”
- If custody/parenting overlap: questions about occupancy and who resides at property, whether children were affected, and how living arrangements were handled.
- Questions about repairs: “Did you notify the landlord?” “Did you attempt repairs yourself?” (anticipate and have evidence ready)

Document checklist — bring originals plus multiple copies (judge, opposing party, your copy)
- Lease/rental agreement (signed copy)
- Rent ledger or payment history (bank deposits, rent checks, receipts)
- All notices served (3-day pay/quit, 30/60-day, cure notices) with proof of service (certificate of mailing, personal service affidavit)
- Correspondence: emails, text message threads, letters (print with timestamps)
- Photographs and videos with dates and labels (move-in condition, damages, repairs)
- Repair receipts, invoices, contractor estimates
- Move-in and move-out inspection reports, security deposit accounting
- Police reports, code enforcement reports, inspection reports (if applicable)
- Discovery responses, deposition transcripts, pleadings, and prior court filings
- Witness statements or declarations (signed and dated)
- Any receipts for expenses or costs you’re claiming (cleaning, locksmith, storage)
- Chronology/timeline (one-page timeline with key dates)

How to prepare your testimony
- Create a concise timeline (one page) with exact dates and documents referenced.
- Practice short, factual answers; avoid volunteering extra information.
- Be honest — inconsistencies undermine credibility.
- If you don’t know or don’t remember, say so — don’t guess.
- Review your discovery answers and documents so testimony matches what’s already in the record.
- Mark and organize exhibits in the order you’ll use them; label copies for judge and opposing party.

Hearing-day checklist
- Bring at least 3 sets of exhibits: judge, opposing counsel/party, your file (more if multiple witnesses).
- Binder with exhibit index and tabbed exhibits; include a one-page chronology.
- Bring originals of critical documents (leases, notices, proof of service).
- Arrive early; allow time for security and locating courtroom.
- Dress neatly; be respectful and speak clearly and directly to the judge.
- Keep phone on silent and do not take calls in the courtroom.

Questions to ask Maria Santos right away (short list to read in person or email)
- Can you confirm the court and courtroom, hearing date/time, and expected time allocation?
- Will you be attending and speaking for me at the hearing? If not, what preparation do you need from me?
