I can help you get organized and make the most of your time with Maria Santos. Below is a focused checklist and set of suggested questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic), plus likely questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask and practical preparation tips. If you want, I can also draft a short email to Maria Santos that lists these questions for you.

First — one quick clarification I should flag
- You said this is in small claims court. In California small claims, parties generally represent themselves at the hearing (attorneys may advise beforehand but usually can’t appear on your behalf in the small claims hearing). Confirm with Maria whether this is actually small claims or an unlawful detainer/other civil court matter. That affects procedure and what you should prepare.

Questions to ask your attorney (high priority)
- Case posture & logistics
  - Can you confirm the current status and the next procedural deadlines (hearing date, scheduling orders)?
  - Is the upcoming hearing in-person or remote? How long will the hearing be scheduled for?
  - Will you be attending the hearing with me, or will you only prepare me to represent myself in court?
  - Are there any pre-hearing filings or exhibit lists I must submit? Deadlines?

- Evidence & documents
  - Which documents will the judge consider most important in this eviction case?
  - Do you recommend I bring originals, copies, or both? How many copies should I bring for the judge/opposing party?
  - How should I authenticate texts, emails, photos, videos, bank records, rent ledgers, or repair invoices?
  - Are there documents I should get subpoenaed (bank records, phone records, property management records)? If so, how and when?

- Notices, service, and statutory compliance
  - Which notices did I need to give (pay-or-quit, cure/quit, 30/60-day termination, etc.) and did I follow the statute’s requirements? How will the judge evaluate proof of service?
  - If opposing counsel says a notice was defective, what proof will the court want?

- Witnesses and testimony
  - Who should I call as witnesses? Do we need witness declarations or live testimony?
  - How should witnesses be prepared? (topics, order, what to expect on cross-examination)
  - Do we need expert witnesses or inspection reports?

- Evidence objections & admissibility
  - What hearsay problems might arise (e.g., third-party statements in emails/texts) and how can we address them?
  - Can rental ledgers, receipts, or business records be admitted under a business-records exception? Do we need a witness to certify them?
  - What is the best way to introduce photos, videos, and repair estimates?

- Strategy & likely legal issues
  - What legal theory will the judge be asked to apply (nonpayment, breach, nuisance, holdover)? What elements must we prove?
  - What are the strengths and weaknesses of our case and of the opposing party’s likely defenses (habitability, retaliatory eviction, payment disputes, offsets)?
  - What settlement options do you recommend (payment plan, agreed move-out, release)? Any standard settlement terms to propose?

- Cross-examination & hostile questions
  - What questions should I expect from opposing counsel and how should I answer them concisely?
  - What admissions should I avoid volunteering? What facts are best to emphasize about timeline and proof?

- Practical and post-judgment matters
  - If we win, how is the judgment enforced (writ of possession, garnishment, levies) and what are typical timeframes?
  - If we lose (or partially win), what are our appeal options or motion options, and what are the deadlines?
  - Are there any confidentiality or protective-order concerns because the opposing party is the other parent in a custody case?

What the judge is likely to ask (prepare short, factual answers)
- What relief are you asking for and how did you calculate the amount?
- What is the timeline of key events (move-in/move-out, notices served, rent due/delays)?
- How and when did you serve the notice(s)? Can you show proof of service?
- Did the tenant/payor ever pay rent or make partial payments? If so, what receipts or bank records show it?
- Were repairs requested or the property uninhabitable? If so, what steps did you take?
- What attempts did you make to resolve the dispute before court?

What opposing counsel may ask (cross-examination themes)
- Inconsistencies in dates and timeline
- Whether notices were properly served or contained required information
- Questions about your motives (e.g., eviction for reasons other than nonpayment)
- Attempts to mitigate damages or accept partial payment
- Credibility attacks (missing receipts, gaps in documentary evidence)
- Questions tying this dispute to the custody matter (if relevant) — attorney should advise how to handle these.

Document/evidence checklist to bring (organize in a labeled binder)
- Lease/rental agreement (signed page)
- Rent ledger showing amounts due, payments, balance
- All notices (pay-or-quit, 3-day, 30/60-day), with proof of service (signed receipts, certified mail, process server declarations)
- Bank statements, rent receipts, canceled checks showing payments
- All communications: emails, texts, written messages (with dates/timestamps); print and also have originals on your phone if possible
- Repair invoices, estimates, inspection reports, code violations
- Photos/videos with dates (ideally timestamped)
- Move-in/move-out checklists, inventory, condition photos
- Any police reports, restraining orders, or court orders relevant to the tenancy
- Witness contact info and a short summary of each witness’s expected testimony

Practical preparations and courtroom tips
- Create a one-page timeline and a one-page “what I want the judge to know” summary to present to your attorney and to bring to court.
- Label and tab exhibits (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2) and prepare at least 3 copies: judge, opposing party, your file (check local court policy; bring more if unsure).
- Practice your opening summary: 2–3 minutes that state the facts, relief sought, and top supporting evidence.
- Practice answers to tough questions with your attorney; keep responses short and factual.
- Be truthful, stay calm, don’t interrupt, and don’t volunteer extra information.
- Arrive early, dress neatly, bring photo ID, and have copies of everything.

Questions about confidentiality and sensitive family issues
- Because the opposing party is the other parent in a custody matter, ask Maria how (if at all) custody-related information can or should be used in this eviction case, and whether any protective orders or redactions are needed.
- Ask if anything you say in the eviction hearing could affect the custody case or vice versa.

Possible procedural issues to confirm with your attorney
- Will the court require you to pre-mark exhibits or submit an exhibit list in advance?
- Are there page limits or formatting requirements for any filings?
- Deadlines and method for issuing subpoenas (witness or document) and who pays for them.
- Rules about bringing electronic evidence or showing video on the court’s equipment.

Next steps I can help with
- Draft a concise email to Maria Santos listing the above questions prioritized for a short meeting.
- Create a one-page timeline or exhibit list template you can fill in.
- Help you script and practice answers to likely judge/opposing counsel questions.

