This is a good place to start — I can help you organize everything so you can be confident at the next stage. Before I give a detailed checklist and suggested questions, one quick clarification please:

- You said “landlord-tenant eviction case” and “small claims court,” but in California eviction (unlawful detainer) cases are usually in Superior Court while small claims handles monetary claims. Which court is this actually filed in?
- You also said the opposing party is “the other parent in the custody case.” Is the tenant the same person as the co‑parent, or did you mean a different opposing party? Any relationship that could affect testimony or credibility is important to know.

If you want, I can also draft an email to Maria Santos with these questions. Below are suggested questions to ask your attorney, what documents to prepare, likely lines of questioning from the judge and opposing counsel, and practical prep tips.

Questions to ask your attorney
(ask Maria to be specific where possible)

Case status & strategy
- Confirm the court, case number, and whether this is unlawful detainer (Superior Court) or small claims. Who is allowed to appear (will you or your attorney appear)?
- What is the legal theory and elements we must prove at hearing (possession, amount owed, damages, breach of lease, notice compliance)?
- What is our strategy and the likely defenses the other side will raise?
- What is our best- and worst-case outcome? What remedies can the judge order?

Evidence & documents
- Please review my evidence and tell me what is missing or weak.
- What documents should I bring to court as exhibits? How many copies should I bring (judge, opposing party, me)?
- Are any documents we plan to use inadmissible or likely to be excluded? What can we do to preserve admissibility?
- For any hearsay items (texts, third-party statements), how should we lay foundation or authenticate them?

Discovery & admissions
- From discovery responses, what admissions or statements by the opposing party help our case?
- Are there any outstanding discovery issues or motions we should file before the hearing?
- Do we need witness declarations or affidavits filed before the hearing?

Specific procedural/logistical questions
- When/where is the hearing scheduled and how long will the hearing take? Are time limits applied?
- What are the local court rules for exhibit labeling, filing exhibits in advance, and courtroom technology (projector, exhibits electronically)?
- Do we need to file anything (exhibit list, witness list) in advance?
- If the other party doesn’t appear, what happens (default, bench warrant, judgment)?

Witnesses & testimony
- Which witnesses should we call and what will each testify to? Should we use sworn declarations instead?
- How should I prepare for cross-examination? Can we do a mock Q&A or role play?
- Are there credibility issues we should address in advance (criminal history, prior inconsistent statements)?

Anticipated lines of questioning (what judge or opposing counsel may ask)
- Tenancy basics: Who is on the lease? When did tenancy start? Who lives at the property? What is the agreed rent amount and due date?
- Notices: Which notices were given (3-day pay/quit, 30/60-day, cure notices)? When and how were they served? Do you have proof of service?
- Payments & ledger: What is the rent ledger? Were payments made late or partial? Do you have bank records, cancelled checks, receipts?
- Repairs & habitability: Did tenant report repairs? Did landlord respond? Do you have repair invoices, photos, contractor statements?
- Damages & cleaning: What damages are being claimed and how was the cost calculated? Do you have estimates/receipts?
- Possession timeline: When did the tenant vacate (or fail to vacate)? When did you regain possession? Any move-out inspection records?
- Communication: Do you have text messages, emails, or written notices? Were any verbal agreements made?
- Credibility & motive: Are there disputes about prior statements, motives, or relationship to the tenant (e.g., co-parent conflict)? Expect questions that probe bias and consistency.
- Financial hardship or mitigation: Did the tenant try to remedy or pay? Did you offer reasonable accommodations or payment plans?

Documents and exhibits to prepare (organization tips)
Bring organized, tabbed packets with originals + copies (judge, opposing counsel, you). Suggested items:
- Lease or rental agreement and any amendments
- Rent ledger showing payments and outstanding balance
- Copies of all notices (3-day, pay/quit, cure/quit, notice to vacate) and proof of service
- Complaint and all pleadings, plus discovery responses and key discovery admissions
- Photos/videos of property condition (with dates annotated)
- Repair invoices, receipts, contractor statements
- Communication logs: texts, emails, written notices (printed with time stamps), call log notes
- Move‑in/move‑out inspection reports and keys inventory
- Security deposit documentation (receipts, accounting)
- Witness contact info and written declarations (signed/dated)
- Any police or code enforcement reports
- Financial proof (bank statements, canceled checks) for payments or damages
- A one-page timeline of key events (dates and short descriptions) to refer to in court
Label each exhibit (Exhibit A, B, C) and include an exhibit list.

What to practice with your attorney
- A short opening statement you can deliver (if you will speak) summarizing your claim and remedy requested.
- Clear, concise answers to the basic questions (who, what, when, where, how). Practice short, factual responses — judges prefer concise facts.
- Mock cross-examination focusing on likely attack points: missed notices, inconsistent dates, poor documentation, alleged habitability issues, motive from custody dispute.
- Handling difficult questions: stay calm, pause if you need to think, don’t argue with counsel or volunteer information beyond the question.

Questions about settlement & alternatives
- Are we willing to settle? If yes, what are acceptable terms (money, move-out date, release)?
- Should we propose mediation or settlement conference before the hearing?
- If settlement is reached, how do we document it to avoid future disputes?

Post-judgment enforcement & costs
- If we win, how will judgment be enforced (writ of possession, wage garnishment, bank levy)? What is the timeline and cost?
- Will we be able to recover court costs, attorney fees, or damages? What is the likelihood of collection?
- If we lose, what are the grounds and the deadlines to appeal or move for reconsideration?

Questions specific to your situation to ask Maria
- How does the prior custody relationship between me and the opposing party affect this eviction case? Could it be used against me (bias, harassment claims)?
- From discovery, which admissions by the other side are strongest for us? Which responses are weak for us?
- Are there any local San Diego procedures or judge preferences I should know about?
- Will you appear at the hearing or prepare me to appear? If you won’t appear, what will you prepare for me (script, exhibits, witness statements)?

Courtroom conduct and testimony tips
- Speak only when asked, answer verbally and directly, pause to think, avoid shouting or arguing.
- Use dates and concrete numbers where possible. If you don’t know, say “I don’t know” rather than guessing.
- Bring multiple sets of exhibits and your timeline. Give copies to the judge and opposing counsel early.
- Dress neatly and arrive early. Bring a notebook and pen.

Next steps I can help with
- I can draft an email to Maria Santos with the list of questions and the court/document checklist.
- I can create a one-page timeline template you can fill in and print for court.
- I can help draft short witness declarations or a script/mock Q&A for practicing answers.

Tell me:
1) Which court this is (Superior/unlawful detainer vs. small claims), and confirm the relationship with the opposing party. 
2) If you want, I’ll draft a concise email to Maria with the questions and a request for a mock session and clarification about whether she will appear at the hearing.

