- Complaint and all pleadings, plus discovery responses and key discovery admissions
- Photos/videos of property condition (with dates annotated)
- Repair invoices, receipts, contractor statements
- Communication logs: texts, emails, written notices (printed with time stamps), call log notes
- Move‑in/move‑out inspection reports and keys inventory
- Security deposit documentation (receipts, accounting)
- Witness contact info and written declarations (signed/dated)
- Any police or code enforcement reports
- Financial proof (bank statements, canceled checks) for payments or damages
- A one-page timeline of key events (dates and short descriptions) to refer to in court
Label each exhibit (Exhibit A, B, C) and include an exhibit list.

What to practice with your attorney
- A short opening statement you can deliver (if you will speak) summarizing your claim and remedy requested.
- Clear, concise answers to the basic questions (who, what, when, where, how). Practice short, factual responses — judges prefer concise facts.
- Mock cross-examination focusing on likely attack points: missed notices, inconsistent dates, poor documentation, alleged habitability issues, motive from custody dispute.
