B. Likely questions — judge and opposing counsel (practice concise answers)
Note: keep answers factual, short, and supported by documents.

1) Tenancy & authority
- “What is the tenant/landlord relationship? Was there a written lease? When did tenancy start?”
- “Who signed the lease? Who lives there now?”
- Documents to cite: lease, signature pages, ID.

2) Notice & compliance
- “When and how did you give the required notice (e.g., 3‑day pay/quit, 30/60 day)? Was it properly served?”
- “Did the tenant receive the notice? Did they respond?”
- Documents: copy of notice, proof of service (signed return, certified mail, process server affidavit), timestamps.

3) Rent / money owed
- “How much is owed and how did you calculate it? What period does that cover?”
- “Are there receipts, bank records, or a rent ledger showing payments and arrears?”
- Documents: rent ledger, bank statements, receipts, canceled checks.

4) Repairs / habitability / defenses
- “Has the tenant complained about habitability or withheld rent? Were there repair requests, code violations, or inspections?”
- “Did you make repairs or offer remedies?”
- Documents: repair invoices, maintenance requests/emails/texts, code inspection reports, dated photos.

5) Possession & conduct
- “Do you seek possession? Did you lock-change or attempt to regain possession? Were there any lockouts or self-help attempts?”
- “Is there damage or other breaches (unauthorized occupants, pets, subletting, illegal activity)?”
- Documents: photos of damage, move-out/move-in inspection, police reports if applicable.

6) Communications & agreements
- “Were there any verbal agreements, payment plans, or partial payments accepted?”
- “Did you ever accept rent after serving notice?”
- Documents: texts, emails, voicemail transcripts, bank transfers.

7) Service & procedural
- “How and when was the complaint served? Were there any service challenges?”
- Documents: proof of service, affidavit.

8) Mitigation & effort to re-rent (if applicable)
- “If the tenant moved out, did you attempt to mitigate damages (re-rent promptly)?”
- Documents: listing ads, showing records, communications with prospective tenants.

9) Credibility and background
- “Have you had prior eviction cases? Any related litigation (e.g., custody) that may be relevant?”
- Be prepared to explain prior cases succinctly; advise attorney if custody matters might be raised so she can object to relevance.

10) Remedies & calculations
- “How did you compute claimed damages (back rent, cleaning, repairs, utilities, late fees)?”
- Provide math and source invoices/receipts.

