- “When and how did you give the required notice (e.g., 3‑day pay/quit, 30/60 day)? Was it properly served?”
- “Did the tenant receive the notice? Did they respond?”
- Documents: copy of notice, proof of service (signed return, certified mail, process server affidavit), timestamps.

3) Rent / money owed
- “How much is owed and how did you calculate it? What period does that cover?”
- “Are there receipts, bank records, or a rent ledger showing payments and arrears?”
- Documents: rent ledger, bank statements, receipts, canceled checks.

4) Repairs / habitability / defenses
- “Has the tenant complained about habitability or withheld rent? Were there repair requests, code violations, or inspections?”
- “Did you make repairs or offer remedies?”
- Documents: repair invoices, maintenance requests/emails/texts, code inspection reports, dated photos.

5) Possession & conduct
- “Do you seek possession? Did you lock-change or attempt to regain possession? Were there any lockouts or self-help attempts?”
- “Is there damage or other breaches (unauthorized occupants, pets, subletting, illegal activity)?”
- Documents: photos of damage, move-out/move-in inspection, police reports if applicable.

6) Communications & agreements
- “Were there any verbal agreements, payment plans, or partial payments accepted?”
- “Did you ever accept rent after serving notice?”
- Documents: texts, emails, voicemail transcripts, bank transfers.

7) Service & procedural
- “How and when was the complaint served? Were there any service challenges?”
- Documents: proof of service, affidavit.

8) Mitigation & effort to re-rent (if applicable)
- “If the tenant moved out, did you attempt to mitigate damages (re-rent promptly)?”
- Documents: listing ads, showing records, communications with prospective tenants.

9) Credibility and background
- “Have you had prior eviction cases? Any related litigation (e.g., custody) that may be relevant?”
- Be prepared to explain prior cases succinctly; advise attorney if custody matters might be raised so she can object to relevance.

10) Remedies & calculations
- “How did you compute claimed damages (back rent, cleaning, repairs, utilities, late fees)?”
- Provide math and source invoices/receipts.

C. Evidence & documents checklist (bring originals + 2–4 photocopies; organized and tabbed)
Essential items:
- Lease / rental agreement and any amendments
- All notices served (3‑day, 30/60, termination) and proof of service
- Complaint/court filings and discovery responses
- Rent ledger showing dates & amounts paid and owed
- Bank records, canceled checks, PayPal/Venmo/transfer receipts
- All communications (emails, texts, letters) printed with timestamps and saved originals/screenshots
- Photos/videos of unit condition (with dates) and damage estimates/invoices/receipts
- Repair estimates, paid invoices, contractor receipts
- Police reports, code enforcement or inspection reports (if any)
- Witness contact info and short written statements (if witnesses will attend)
- Timeline one-pager (concise chronology of key events)
- Exhibit list (numbered or lettered), and copies for judge, opposing counsel, and yourself
- Discovery documents produced by the other side that support your case
- Any settlement offers or release proposals

D. Practical courtroom prep, strategy, and conduct
- Organize a one‑page timeline and a one‑page “what I will say” bullet list to keep answers short.
- Label exhibits (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2…), staple a cover sheet with a short description, bring at least 3 copies.
- Practice concise answers with Maria; do quick mock Q&A focusing on the judge’s plain questions. Avoid volunteering extra info.
- Decide in advance what your opening statement will be (30–60 seconds): the claim and the dollar amount / relief requested.
- Be ready for opposing counsel to try to introduce irrelevant facts (e.g., custody issues). Ask Maria about making timely objections or asking the judge to keep those issues out as irrelevant.
- Know the standard of proof: preponderance of evidence (more likely than not) for civil claims.
- Courtroom demeanor: be punctual, dress neatly, be respectful, and answer the judge directly and briefly.
- Ask Maria whether small claims in your instance allows attorney presence and speaking — in California small claims the plaintiff often speaks for themselves or may have limited attorney involvement; confirm strategy with Maria.

E. Questions to anticipate from opposing counsel (for discovery or at trial)
- Requests for admissions about dates, payments, or statements you made
- Attempts to impeach credibility (contradictory dates, inconsistent statements)
- Questions designed to show you accepted rent or waived notices
- Questions about motive (retaliation, discrimination) or unrelated personal matters
Ask Maria how to respond or object to these lines of questioning and whether to move to exclude them.

F. Follow-ups to ask Maria after your list/questions
- Can we role-play a 10–15 minute mock hearing? I want feedback on tone and content.
- Are there particular exhibits you want prepped as priority exhibits in court?
- If the other side brings new documents at hearing, how should we respond?
- What is your recommended settlement threshold (amount or non-monetary terms) and authorization to settle?

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short email to Maria with the prioritized questions above.
- Create a one‑page timeline from facts you give me.
- Draft an exhibit list template you can fill in and print.

Which of those would you like me to do next?

