I can help with that. A few quick clarifying questions so I tailor this to your situation:

- Are you the landlord or the tenant in this eviction case?
- Is the case actually in small claims court (monetary-only claims) or in Superior Court as an unlawful detainer/eviction? (Evictions are normally handled in Superior Court in California; small claims is for money damages.)
- When you say the opposing party is “the other parent in the custody case,” do you mean the same person is the tenant/opposing party here?
- Do you want a short list of questions to ask Maria Santos, or do you also want suggested answers/practice questions you can rehearse (questions the judge or opposing counsel might ask you)?

Below is a structured set of things to ask your attorney and a list of likely questions the judge or opposing counsel could ask — organized so you can take this into your meeting with Maria.

A. Essential procedural questions to ask your attorney
- Confirm jurisdiction and claim type
  - Is this actually a small claims case or an unlawful detainer (Superior Court) eviction? Which court will hear it in San Diego County?
  - What is the specific legal claim and remedy we are pursuing (possession, back rent, damages, costs)?
- Case status and timeline
  - What is the expected timeline now that discovery is closed? When will the hearing be set?
  - Are there any remaining pre-hearing filings, witness lists, or exhibit lists required by the court?
- Burdens of proof and legal standards
  - What exactly must I prove at the hearing (elements of the claim, standards of evidence)?
  - What defenses is the opposing party most likely to assert?
- Evidence and admissibility
  - Which documents are most important and what form should they be in (originals, copies, certified, timeline)?
  - Any hearsay or foundation issues we should anticipate? How should we authenticate communications (texts, emails)?
- Witnesses and testimony
  - Who should testify for us? Do we need the landlord/property manager/repair person/neighbors to appear?
  - Do we need subpoenas to compel witnesses or documents? If so, please prepare these.
- Exhibits and organization
  - How should we mark exhibits and how many copies do we need for the judge, opposing party, and ourselves?
  - Can we submit a brief pre-hearing exhibit list or witness list to the court? If so, can you draft it?
- Settlement and negotiation
  - What settlement options should I consider, and what is your recommended bottom line?
  - Would the court require or allow mediation before the hearing?
- Practical hearing logistics
  - How long will my hearing likely be? Who will speak and in what order?
  - What courtroom procedures and etiquette should I follow (when to stand, how to address the judge)?
- Costs, fees, and appeals
  - What are the expected court costs and potential attorney fees (if recoverable)?
  - If we lose, what are appeal or post-judgment enforcement options and deadlines?
- Communication and preparation with you (your attorney)
  - Can we do a mock questioning/prep session? How long will that be?
  - Can you prepare a short pre-hearing memo summarizing strategy and key points I must prove?
  - Please confirm how you will present evidence at hearing (you speaking vs. me testifying first).

B. Documents and exhibits to confirm with your attorney (bring these to your meeting and the hearing)
- Lease or rental agreement
- Notices served (e.g., pay or quit, cure/quit, 3/30/60-day notices) with proof of service (certified mail receipts/process server)
- Rent ledger/ledger showing payments, missed payments, partials
- Receipts, canceled checks, bank statements showing rent payment history
- Communications: texts, emails, voicemail logs, repair requests, letters (print with dates/timestamps)
- Photographs and videos (dated) showing conditions, damages, or other relevant facts
- Repair invoices, estimates, receipts, contractors’ statements
- Police reports, inspection reports, code violations, health department records (if habitability is an issue)
- Witness contact info and short witness statements (signed if possible)
- Any court filings and discovery responses exchanged (interrogatories, admissions, production)
- Timeline or chronology document (one-page summary of events with dates)
- Identification and any supporting documents re: occupancy or ownership (if needed)

C. Questions the judge is likely to ask (prepare concise answers)
- What remedy are you asking the court to grant today? (Be specific: possession? back rent amount? damages?)
- Can you briefly explain the timeline of events leading to this claim? (Keep a short oral timeline ready.)
- Did you serve the required notices? When and how were they served?
- How much rent is owed and how did you calculate that amount? (Be ready with a rent ledger)
- Did the tenant/other party raise any reason why the rent should not be paid or the eviction should not proceed?
- Have you tried to resolve this outside of court? What offers or negotiations have occurred?
- Are there any repairs or habitability issues that could affect the claim? How were they handled?
- Do you have witnesses who will testify? What will they confirm?
- Are you seeking attorney’s fees or costs and on what basis?

D. Questions opposing counsel might ask you at hearing (prepare concise, truthful answers)
- Why did you fail to pay rent or perform your obligations (if you are the tenant)?
- When and how were notices given to you? Did you receive them?
- Can you corroborate the dates and amounts you are claiming (receipts, bank records)?
- Who else knew about or participated in this matter? (co-tenants, property manager)
- Were any repairs requested? Did the landlord respond? (show records)
- Have you made any efforts to mitigate damages or reach a resolution?
- If contesting an eviction: did you withhold rent for repairs? What was the basis (habitability code)?
- If the landlord: did you follow the exact statutory procedures for eviction and service of notice?

E. Strategic and evidentiary issues to discuss with your attorney
- Authentication of electronic communications: how to lay foundation for emails/texts (witness testimony + metadata).
- Best way to present a timeline — a one-page exhibit can be powerful and easy for the judge.
- How to handle hearsay or business records objections (get business records affidavit or witness).
- Impeachment preparation: if opposing party will contradict prior statements or discovery responses.
- Whether to call expert witnesses (for repairs/damage valuation) or rely on invoices/estimates.
- Whether to request the judge to inspect the property (rare but possible) or submit photos instead.
- Whether to ask the court for prejudgment interest, costs, or attorney’s fees and the legal basis.

F. Practical prep and courtroom behavior tips to confirm with counsel
- Practice a 2–3 minute opening statement summarizing key facts and relief requested.
- Prepare short direct-examination scripts and likely cross-examination questions for yourself.
- Don’t volunteer extra facts; answer questions briefly and honestly.
- If you don’t know an answer, say so and offer to follow up rather than guessing.
- Mark and label exhibits in advance; bring sets for the court, opposing counsel, and yourself.
- Dress professionally, arrive early, and bring extra copies of everything.

G. Example short script requests you can ask Maria Santos
- “Maria, can we do a 60–90 minute pre-hearing prep where we run through likely direct and cross questions, and mark the exhibits?”
- “Please prepare a one-page pre-hearing memo for me that includes the timeline, the three strongest facts in our favor, the three weaknesses, and our settlement bottom line.”
- “Can you confirm which documents we should admit by stipulation and which will require testimony to authenticate?”

If you like, I can:
- Draft a one-page hearing timeline you can bring to your attorney and the court.
- Draft an email to Maria Santos summarizing these questions and requesting a mock-prep session.
- Create a short practice Q&A script (questions the judge/opposing counsel might ask and concise responses you can rehearse) tailored to whether you are the landlord or tenant.

Tell me the clarifying answers (landlord vs tenant, small claims vs unlawful detainer, whether you want a draft email or practice script) and I’ll prepare the next item you want.

