I can help you get organized. Below is a targeted checklist you can use to prepare for the hearing and a list of focused questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos). I’ve also included likely questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask and practical prep steps you can take now.

First — one quick clarification to confirm with Maria
- Confirm the exact case type and court: you mentioned “small claims” and an eviction — in California most evictions are handled as unlawful detainer in Superior Court (not small claims). Confirm whether this is small claims, unlawful detainer, or another proceeding in San Diego County and whether attorneys will be permitted to appear on your behalf in the hearing. That affects procedure and strategy.

Checklist of documents and evidence to have ready
- Lease or rental agreement (all pages, signed)
- Notice(s) given (notice to pay or quit, notice to cure, 30/60-day notices, notice of termination)
- Rent ledger / payment history (dates, amounts paid, outstanding balance)
- Copies of checks, bank records, money orders, PayPal/ACH receipts
- Communications: texts, emails, written notices, log of phone calls (dates + summary)
- Photographs and videos (with dates) showing condition, repairs, or issues
- Repair invoices, receipts, contractor/handyman estimates
- Inspection reports, code enforcement records, letters from building management
- Witness contact info and short written statements (neighbors, contractors, property manager)
- Any prior court orders, case numbers, pleadings, and discovery responses
- Evidence of attempts to mitigate damages (e.g., advertising to re-rent, offers to fix)
- ID and documents verifying occupancy/tenancy (if identity or custody is relevant)
- Timeline (one-page chronological summary of key events and dates)

Questions to ask Maria (legal/strategic)
- Confirm case type and timeline:
  - What exact court and department will hear this? When can we expect the hearing date?
  - What is the expected length/duration of our hearing?
- Representation and procedure:
  - Am I allowed to have you (Maria) speak for me in court, or will I need to speak for myself?
  - Will we submit any pre-hearing filings or witness lists? If so, what and when?
- Evidence & exhibits:
  - Which of my documents do you consider strongest? Which may be weak or excluded?
  - How should exhibits be labeled and submitted? Do we need multiple copies for the judge/opposing counsel?
  - Are there any authentication or foundation issues you foresee (e.g., hearsay problems)?
- Witnesses:
  - Which witnesses should we call? What will you want them to say?
  - Do we need subpoenas to ensure witness attendance or to get records (bank records, phone records)?
- Opposing claims & defenses:
  - What defenses or counterclaims might the other side raise (retaliation, habitability, improper notice)?
  - What admissions of mine or potential inconsistencies could the other side use? How should I respond or avoid them?
- Questions to expect:
  - What specific questions do you expect the judge and opposing counsel to ask me or the witnesses?
  - How should I answer sensitive questions (about rent, payment history, living arrangements, communications)?
- Settlement/negotiation:
  - What settlement options are on the table? What is our minimum acceptable outcome?
  - Would mediation be advisable before hearing?
- Burden and standard:
  - What is the burden of proof for each claim/defense and what does the judge need to find to rule for us?
- Post-judgment:
  - If we win, what are my remedies and how will they be enforced in San Diego County?
  - If we lose, what are the grounds and timelines for appeal or post-judgment motions?
- Costs and fees:
  - Are we seeking costs, filing fees, or attorney fees? What documentation is needed to support this?
- Practical prep:
  - Can we do a mock Q&A or run through the timeline together? Can you coach me on concise answers?

Likely questions the judge may ask (prepare short factual answers)
- What is the claim you’re making and what remedy are you asking the court for?
- When did the tenancy begin and what is the nature of the agreement (written/oral)?
- Was a proper notice served — when and how was it served?
- How much rent is owed (if any) — show calculation and dates
- What efforts did you make to notify or resolve the issue before filing?
- For habitability/repairs: When did you report the problem, what was the response, and what harm resulted?
- For eviction: Did the tenant/occupant vacate? If not, why should the court order possession?
- For damages: Show receipts and explain how you calculated damages or costs incurred.

Common lines of questioning from opposing counsel (what they’ll try to establish)
- Attack credibility: inconsistent statements, gaps in your timeline, missing documentation
- Mitigation: why didn’t you take steps to reduce damages (re-rent, repair, etc.)?
- Technical compliance: whether notices were legally sufficient (timing, method of service)
- Causation: link between the alleged breach or damage and the claimed losses
- Counterclaims: attempt to shift blame (e.g., alleging you caused issues, failed maintenance, discrimination, or retaliatory actions)

How to prepare your answers and testimony
- Keep answers short, factual, and date-specific. Avoid speculation or volunteering extra details.
- Practice a concise opening statement summarizing your position and requested remedy (30–60 seconds).
- Prepare plain-language explanations for technical items (e.g., how you calculated owed rent).
- If unsure of an answer, say you don’t recall and offer to refer to the exhibit or your timeline — don’t guess.
- Rehearse with Maria: she can do a mock cross-examination to expose potential weaknesses.

Evidentiary and procedural items to check with Maria
- Which documents need foundation/authentication and how will we establish it?
- Do any documents risk exclusion as hearsay? If so, can we use an exception or a witness to authenticate?
- Are there any pre-hearing deadlines (exchange of exhibits, witness lists, motions in limine)?
- How will exhibits be presented in court (paper copies, electronic, binder)?
- Will we need a demonstrative timeline or summary exhibit for the judge?

Practical courtroom tips
- Arrive early, dress conservatively, bring extra copies of exhibits and your timeline.
- Address the judge as “Your Honor.” Speak clearly, and pause before answering.
- If your attorney is speaking, don’t interrupt. If you must clarify, wait for your turn.
- Keep emotions in check — judges focus on facts and credibility.
- If a question is unclear, ask for clarification rather than guessing.

Next steps you can take now (actionable)
- Confirm case type and whether attorney will appear for you in court.
- Create a one-page timeline of events with dates and a shorter 2–3 sentence summary for the judge.
- Gather and label your exhibits in chronological order.
- Send the exhibit list and timeline to Maria and ask for a prep session (mock Q&A).
- Verify hearing logistics (date, time, courtroom, estimated length).

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a concise email to Maria listing these questions and requesting a prep meeting and confirmation of court type/logistics.
- Create a one-page timeline template you can fill in.
Which of those would help you most right now?

