- Move-in and move-out inspection reports, condition photos or videos (with dates)
- Receipts/invoices for repairs, cleaning, or damage repairs
- Security deposit receipts and any accounting for deductions
- Photos/videos of alleged damage or habitability issues (with timestamps)
- Police reports, 311 reports, code enforcement reports (if applicable)
- Witness contact info and short written statements (neighbors, contractors)
- Any prior court filings, discovery responses, deposition transcripts, and the discovery documents you already produced/received

Possible judge/opposing counsel questions (practice short answers)
Questions about notice and timing
- “When did you give the tenant/landlord notice?” — Have the date, type of notice, and proof of service ready.
- “How long did you wait after giving notice?” — State dates and reference the notice.

Questions about money and damages
- “How much are you claiming and how did you calculate it?” — Show the math with receipts, ledger, and invoices.
- “Did you mitigate damages?” (If applicable) — Explain reasonable steps you took to reduce loss.

Questions about repairs, habitability, or damage
- “When did you first learn of the problem and what did you do?” — Provide timeline and copies of repair requests and receipts.
- “Who completed the repairs and what did they charge?” — Provide contractor invoices and before/after photos.

Credibility and relationship issues
- “Have you discussed this issue with the other party? What was their response?” — Summarize communications and produce copies.
- “Why didn’t you follow [X step]?” — Answer succinctly, and explain any constraints (e.g., safety concerns, inability to access unit).

Custody/other-case overlap (you mentioned the opposing party is the other parent)
- Ask Maria whether any custody-related facts are relevant to occupancy or rights in this tenancy case and whether those documents should be disclosed.
- Be prepared to explain succinctly why the custody matter is or is not related to tenancy issues.

Practical preparation tips (how to present at the hearing)
- Create a one-page timeline of key events with dates (bring copies for judge/opposing party).
- Create an exhibit index: numbered tabs, description, and date. Provide 2–3 sets: judge, other side, you.
- Practice concise answers (one- or two-sentence responses). Do a mock Q&A with Maria or a friend.
- Bring a binder with originals, plus photocopies in separate folders: pleadings, discovery, exhibits, witness declarations.
