- “What notice did you give and when was it served?” — Produce the notice and proof of service; be ready to say the exact date and who served it.
- “How much are you seeking and how did you calculate it?” — Bring a clear spreadsheet and receipts; state the total and the components (rent months, late fees, repairs).
- “Have you attempted to mitigate damages or re-rent?” — Explain any efforts to mitigate (ads, showing the unit) and show relevant proof (advertisements, communications).
- “Were there repairs or habitability issues?” — Be prepared with evidence and explain communications/repair timeline.
- “Are there any counterclaims?” — Ask your attorney to summarize the other party’s counterclaims and be ready to respond.

Likely opposing counsel or opposing party questions
- “Did you accept any partial payments?” — Have payment records ready and be honest; partial payments may affect amounts owed.
- “Did you ever promise not to evict or accept late payments?” — Produce written proof; avoid relying only on memory.
- “Who else lived in the property and when?” — Have names and dates.
- “Why did you withhold repair funds (if claiming habitability)?” — Provide repair estimates, notices to landlord/tenant, and receipts.
- “Is this related to your custody case? Are you using this litigation strategically?” — Coordinate with your attorney about how to answer and whether to avoid discussing custody details.

