I can help you get organized and prepare the right questions for Maria Santos and to rehearse likely lines of questioning at the hearing. A couple quick clarifying points before the checklist: are you the landlord or the tenant (plaintiff or defendant)? And is this actually a small‑claims matter, or an unlawful detainer/eviction in superior court? Those details change some of the procedure and remedies. If you don’t know, that’s fine — I’ll give a broadly useful list you can use either way.

Below are (A) specific questions to ask your attorney, grouped by topic; (B) likely questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask and short sample answers/ways to practice them; (C) documents and how to organize them for the hearing; and (D) hearing and courtroom tips plus practical next steps.

A — Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
Process & status
- What is the current status and next court dates? Do we have a confirmed hearing date or are we still waiting?
- Is this being handled in small claims or superior court (unlawful detainer)? What different rules apply for our court?
- What is the standard of proof the judge will use in this hearing?

Case theory & evidence
- Walk me through exactly what we have to prove (or disprove) at the hearing. What elements are essential?
- Which documents or pieces of evidence are critical, and which are merely supportive?
- Do you expect any evidentiary objections from opposing counsel? How will we handle hearsay, authentication of texts/photos, or business records?

Witnesses & subpoenas
- Which witnesses should we have testify (myself, tenants/co‑tenants, repair contractors, neighbors, police, property manager)?
- Do we need subpoenas for any witnesses or records? If so, who and when will you serve them?

Strategy & testimony
- Will you be representing me in court for the hearing, or will I need to speak? If you’ll speak, what parts will I need to testify about?
- Do you recommend I testify? If yes, what are the exact facts I should emphasize and what should I avoid saying?
- What settlement range is reasonable? What is my minimum acceptable outcome?

Logistics & local rules
- Are there local San Diego County rules or forms I must file (exhibit list, trial brief, witness list, proof of service)?
- How long is my testimony likely to be? How long is the hearing usually scheduled for?
- Do we need extra copies of exhibits for the judge and opposing counsel? Any electronic exhibit rules?

Costs, fees & post-judgment
- What are the likely court costs, filing fees, and your fees going forward?
- If we win, what remedies or money judgments are likely and how will we collect? If we lose, what are appeal options/timelines?

Preparation
- Can we do a mock Q&A before the hearing? Can you prepare an exhibit list and a short timeline for me to memorize?
- Are there any motions we should file pre‑hearing (e.g., motion in limine, motions about excluded evidence)?

B — Likely questions from the judge or opposing counsel (practice Q&A)
General opening
- Judge: “State your name and who you represent.” — Practice: “My name is [X]. I am the [landlord/tenant/plaintiff/defendant] and represented by Maria Santos of Santos Law Office.” (If counsel speaks for you, confirm that.)

Timeline and documents
- “When did the tenancy begin and end (if it ended)?” — Give exact dates from the lease.
- “What is the lease term and monthly rent amount?” — Know the exact figure and lease clauses (due date, grace period).
- “Were written notices served? When and how?” — Be ready with dates and proof of service (3‑day/30/60 day notices, proof of mailing/hand‑delivery).

Money and damages
- “How much rent is owed (or how much are you seeking) and how did you calculate it?” — Have a rent ledger that sums outstanding amounts and payments, with dates.
- “What specific damages are you claiming (repairs, unpaid rent, cleaning, unpaid utilities) and supporting receipts?” — Be ready to show invoices/receipts.

Habitability/repairs (tenant side)
- “When did you notify the landlord about the problem? How did you notify them?” — Give dates, method (text/email), copies.
- “Did you withhold rent or make repairs yourself? Provide receipts and explain why.” — Show proof and legal basis (ask attorney about rent withholding rules).

Possession & delivery
- “When did the tenant vacate or when was possession returned?” — Provide move‑out date and any evidence (keys, inspection report).
- “Was a lockout or self help attempted?” — If applicable, explain facts and whether lawful steps were followed.

Credibility and communication
- Opposing counsel may ask: “You testified X; can you explain Y discrepancy?” — Don’t be defensive. Practice concise factual answers and point to documents.
- Judge often asks clarifying follow-ups: keep answers short, stick to dates and documents.

Sample short answers to practice:
- Q: “When did you serve the notice?” A: “On May 3, 2025. Here is the proof of service dated May 3, 2025.”
- Q: “How much are you claiming?” A: “$4,250 for unpaid rent from March–June 2025, itemized in this ledger (Exhibit 2).”
- Q: “Did you try to resolve this before filing?” A: “Yes — I sent these texts/emails on April 10 and April 20 asking for payment; no payment was made. (Exhibit 4).”

C — Documents to prepare and how to organize them
Bring organized, labeled copies. Provide 3 sets if possible: judge, opposing counsel, your copy.
Essential documents
- Lease agreement and any written amendments or sublease agreements.
- Rent ledger showing payments, dates, and balance due (spreadsheet or ledger printout).
- Notices (3‑day, 30/60‑day notices) and proof of service.
- Receipts, canceled checks, bank statements showing rent payments or non‑payments.
- Emails, text messages, written communications (print with timestamps). For screenshots, also prepare original metadata if available.
- Photographs and videos with dates/timestamps (habitability or damage).
- Repair invoices, estimates, receipts, contractor statements.
- Move‑in/move‑out condition reports and inspection checklists.
- Police reports, code enforcement citations, or other official documents if relevant.
- Witness contact info and short witness statements/affidavits.
- Any prior court filings, discovery responses, deposition summaries (if applicable).
Organization & labeling
- Table of contents, numbered exhibits (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2…), sticky tabs.
- Highlight key pages (lease clause, amounts due, proof of service).
- Prepare an exhibit list with short descriptions and dates.
- If the court accepts electronic exhibits, bring a USB or uploaded copies per local rules.

D — Courtroom & testimony tips
- Stick to facts. Don’t volunteer extra information or speculate.
- Answer only the question asked. Pause before responding so your attorney can object if needed.
- Use dates, numbers, and names — judges like specifics.
- If you don’t remember, say “I don’t recall” rather than guessing.
- Be calm and professional. Dress neatly, arrive early, bring ID.
- If opposing counsel misstates a fact, correct succinctly and point to the document.
- Practice memory aids: a short timeline (one page) with key dates you can reference while testifying.

E — Practical next steps (what to request from Maria)
- Ask for a mock Q&A and for her to role‑play opposing counsel’s likely lines of questioning.
- Ask her to prepare an exhibit list and help you organize exhibits into labeled copies.
- Confirm who will speak in court, and whether you will testify.
- Confirm if any subpoenas are needed and who will issue them.
- Ask her to identify the top 3 weaknesses and top 3 strengths of your case and to prepare responses for the weaknesses.
- Get a written checklist of documents she wants and a deadline for producing any pre‑hearing filings.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a one‑page printable checklist of exactly which documents to assemble and how to label them?
- Create a mock Q&A script (judge + opposing counsel questions with concise sample answers) tailored for landlord or tenant? 

If you tell me whether you’re the landlord or tenant and whether this is small claims or unlawful detainer (superior court), I’ll tailor the checklist and the mock Q&A specifically to your role and local San Diego practice.

